{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01XF5m7iRbBBmxBBWzMSS19D","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Original framing fully intact. Lead with ''first large-scale\n2\t  psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability'' — the 31.3% finding, the\n3\t  meta-critique, the validation framework, and the diagnostic lens all stand as stated.\n4\t  No claim softening needed. Paper positions DIF as the missing diagnostic layer the\n5\t  evaluation community didn''t know it needed. All 4 claims survive review intact;\n6\t  ''instability ≠ invalidity'' framing is recognized as a genuine conceptual contribution;\n7\t  meta-critique lands as field-wide insight. Reviewers accept ability confound controls,\n8\t  GSM1K corroboration as suggestive, and model independence controls as preemptive.\n9\t  8-page EMNLP compression retains all 4 claims without feeling rushed. Conclusion\n10\t  makes forward-looking recommendations (benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits\n11\t  periodically) without hedging.\n12\t\n13\t  '\n14\timagined_experiments:\n15\t- name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n16\t  plan_id: plan_001\n17\t  description: 'Apply MH-DIF to full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models\n18\t    × 12,508 items). Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, architecture\n19\t    stratification, within-family confound control (Llama-2 vs Llama-3), domain-specificity\n20\t    analysis, and proxy validation against Li et al. web-overlap labels.\n21\t\n22\t    '\n23\t  expected_results:\n24\t    temporal_ets_c_pct: 31.3\n25\t    temporal_enrichment_vs_li: 0.96\n26\t    temporal_fpr_on_li_clean: 0.318\n27\t    ability_quintile_ets_c_pct: 2.9\n28\t    ability_quintile_enrichment: 1.19\n29\t    within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 61.3\n30\t    within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct: 38.7\n31\t    domain_spearman_rho: 0.307\n32\t    domain_spearman_p: 0.02\n33\t    sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.18\n34\t    temporal_ability_jaccard: 0.014\n35\t    n_subjects_with_dif: 57\n36\t    subject_c_pct_range: 20.7–48.4\n37\t    subject_c_pct_unweighted_mean: 30.0\n38\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the temporal cohort analysis cleanly reveals\n39\t    31.3% ETS-C items — a number that is striking enough to command attention but\n40\t    not so extreme as to suggest methodological artifact. The near-unity enrichment\n41\t    (0.96) against Li et al. labels is the keystone of the meta-critique: DIF flags\n42\t    are orthogonal to web-overlap labels, confirming that these measure fundamentally\n43\t    different constructs (differential vs global exposure). The ability-quintile cohort\n44\t    shows only 2.9% C-items — a critical internal control proving that when groups\n45\t    differ only in ability (not temporally), DIF correctly produces near-zero flags.\n46\t    The random-split sanity check at 0.18% eliminates statistical artifact concerns\n47\t    entirely. Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is high because these families have\n48\t    maximal temporal separation AND known architecture/training changes — this becomes\n49\t    a positive result showing DIF captures genuine cross-generation functional shifts.\n50\t    Domain ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) is modest but significant, appropriately handled by plan_019\n51\t    decomposition showing domain improvement explains only 2.4pp. All 57 subjects\n52\t    showing some instability (range 20.7–48.4%) demonstrates this is a benchmark-wide\n53\t    phenomenon, not concentrated in a few pathological domains. Reviewers accept this\n54\t    as the definitive large-scale characterization of MMLU temporal stability.\n55\t\n56\t    '\n57\t  tables:\n58\t  - name: 'Table 1: DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition'\n59\t    columns:\n60\t    - Cohort\n61\t    - N_ref\n62\t    - N_foc\n63\t    - ETS_A%\n64\t    - ETS_B%\n65\t    - ETS_C%\n66\t    - Enrichment\n67\t    rows:\n68\t    - - Temporal (2023 vs 2024-H1)\n69\t      - 1253\n70\t      - 656\n71\t      - '47.8'\n72\t      - '20.9'\n73\t      - '31.3'\n74\t      - 0.96\n75\t    - - Ability (Q1 vs Q5)\n76\t      - 1044\n77\t      - 1044\n78\t      - '89.2'\n79\t      - '7.9'\n80\t      - '2.9'\n81\t      - 1.19\n82\t    - - Random split (sanity)\n83\t      - 960\n84\t      - 960\n85\t      - '97.5'\n86\t      - '2.3'\n87\t      - '0.18'\n88\t      - —\n89\t  - name: 'Table 2: Within-Family DIF Confound Control'\n90\t    columns:\n91\t    - Family pair\n92\t    - N_ref\n93\t    - N_foc\n94\t    - C%\n95\t    - Interpretation\n96\t    rows:\n97\t    - - Llama-2 vs Llama-3\n98\t      - 161\n99\t      - 308\n100\t      - '61.3'\n101\t      - Capability gain + architecture shift\n102\t    - - Mistral vs Mixtral\n103\t      - 134\n104\t      - 89\n105\t      - '38.7'\n106\t      - Architecture shift (dense→MoE)\n107\t  figures_description:\n108\t  - 'Figure 1: Histogram of per-subject ETS-C percentages across 57 MMLU domains,\n109\t    showing all subjects above 20% with unimodal distribution centered near 30%.'\n110\t  - 'Figure 2: Scatter plot of domain accuracy improvement (Δacc) vs DIF C% with ρ=0.307\n111\t    regression line, showing weak positive association.'\n112\t  analysis: 'The 31.3% figure anchors the entire paper. Its robustness is demonstrated\n113\t    through three internal controls: (1) ability-only cohort produces 2.9% — proving\n114\t    temporal signal is not an ability artifact, (2) random split produces 0.18% —\n115\t    proving it is not statistical noise, (3) enrichment ≈ 1.0 — proving DIF flags\n116\t    are orthogonal to web-overlap contamination labels. The within-family results\n117\t    are higher than temporal (61.3% for Llama-2v3) because these compare families\n118\t    with known architecture changes, validating DIF sensitivity to genuine cross-generation\n119\t    shifts. In the best tier, reviewers see this not as \"DIF fails\" but as \"DIF correctly\n120\t    identifies that MMLU items function differently across model generations.\"\n121\t\n122\t    '\n123\t- name: rl_robustness_validation\n124\t  plan_id: plan_002\n125\t  description: 'Validate DIF''s robustness to RL-trained models. SFT-only baseline,\n126\t    alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) mechanism control, and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) focal models.\n127\t    Two-level validation: group-level DIF stability and model-level traditional detector\n128\t    degradation.\n129\t\n130\t    '\n131\t  expected_results:\n132\t    n_sft_baseline: 312\n133\t    n_alignment_rl: 477\n134\t    n_reasoning_rl: 13\n135\t    dif_c_pct_sft_cohort: 30.8\n136\t    dif_c_pct_alignment_rl_cohort: 31.1\n137\t    dif_c_pct_mixed_rl_cohort: 30.5\n138\t    jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.87\n139\t    jaccard_sft_vs_mixed_rl: 0.84\n140\t    constat_auroc_sft: 0.72\n141\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.41\n142\t    mink_auroc_sft: 0.68\n143\t    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.35\n144\t    dif_retention_ratio_vs_constat: 0.97\n145\t    tost_equivalence_p: 0.008\n146\t    bootstrap_ci_dif_auroc: '[0.88, 0.94]'\n147\t    delong_p_dif_vs_constat: 0.003\n148\t    partial_exploitation_boundary_p: 0.45\n149\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, DIF''s group-level nature makes it inherently\n150\t    robust to RL training: MH conditions on total score within the cohort, so individual\n151\t    model training methodology is irrelevant as long as the cohort composition doesn''t\n152\t    systematically shift. With only 13 reasoning-RL models added to a focal group\n153\t    of 656+, the cohort composition barely changes (Jaccard 0.84 with baseline). The\n154\t    C% remains within 1pp of baseline across all three cohort compositions, confirming\n155\t    group-level stability. Meanwhile, ConStat and Min-K% — which operate at the individual\n156\t    model level — degrade sharply on reasoning-RL models (AUROC drops from 0.72/0.68\n157\t    to 0.41/0.35) because GRPO/PPO flatten the NLL landscape these detectors rely\n158\t    on. TOST equivalence testing (p=0.008) formally confirms DIF retention is not\n159\t    just \"failed to reject null\" but positively equivalent. The partial exploitation\n160\t    analysis shows DIF detection boundary at p≈0.45, meaning contamination must affect\n161\t    <45% of focal group to evade — a reasonable operational bound. Reviewers accept\n162\t    the N=13 reasoning-RL sample because DIF''s claim is group-level robustness (adding\n163\t    13 models doesn''t change group statistics), while per-model detector degradation\n164\t    is demonstrated on exactly those 13 models individually.\n165\t\n166\t    '\n167\t  tables:\n168\t  - name: 'Table: DIF Stability Across Cohort Compositions'\n169\t    columns:\n170\t    - Cohort composition\n171\t    - N_focal\n172\t    - C%\n173\t    - Jaccard vs SFT baseline\n174\t    rows:\n175\t    - - SFT-only focal\n176\t      - 312\n177\t      - '30.8'\n178\t      - '1.00'\n179\t    - - + Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO)\n180\t      - 789\n181\t      - '31.1'\n182\t      - '0.87'\n183\t    - - + Reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\n184\t      - 802\n185\t      - '30.5'\n186\t      - '0.84'\n187\t  - name: 'Table: Per-Model Detector Degradation on Reasoning-RL'\n188\t    columns:\n189\t    - Detector\n190\t    - AUROC (SFT)\n191\t    - AUROC (Reasoning-RL)\n192\t    - Δ\n193\t    - DeLong p\n194\t    rows:\n195\t    - - DIF (group-level)\n196\t      - '0.91'\n197\t      - '0.90'\n198\t      - '-0.01'\n199\t      - '0.82'\n200\t    - - ConStat\n201\t      - '0.72'\n202\t      - '0.41'\n203\t      - '-0.31'\n204\t      - '0.003'\n205\t    - - Min-K%\n206\t      - '0.68'\n207\t      - '0.35'\n208\t      - '-0.33'\n209\t      - '0.001'\n210\t  figures_description:\n211\t  - 'Figure: Detection power (AUROC) as function of exploitation rate p∈[0.4,1.0]\n212\t    for DIF, ConStat, and Min-K%. DIF maintains AUROC>0.85 down to p≈0.45; ConStat\n213\t    drops below 0.6 at p=0.7; Min-K% drops below 0.5 at p=0.75.'\n214\t  analysis: 'This experiment serves a dual rhetorical purpose: (1) it shows DIF is\n215\t    robust to the specific threat model (RL concealing contamination signals) that\n216\t    motivates the paper, and (2) it provides a direct head-to-head comparison with\n217\t    existing per-model detectors. In the best tier, the N=13 limitation is acknowledged\n218\t    but reviewers accept the two-level design as sufficient — DIF''s group-level nature\n219\t    means the validation logic is sound even with small RL samples. The partial exploitation\n220\t    analysis adds theoretical depth, characterizing when DIF works rather than just\n221\t    showing that it does.\n222\t\n223\t    '\n224\t- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n225\t  plan_id: plan_003a\n226\t  description: 'Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+\n227\t    MMLU items. LLM paraphrasing, original vs reformulated comparison, FDR-controlled\n228\t    detection.\n229\t\n230\t    '\n231\t  expected_results:\n232\t    n_items_paraphrased: 247\n233\t    constat_detection_rate_all: 0.38\n234\t    constat_detection_rate_sft: 0.44\n235\t    constat_detection_rate_rl: 0.12\n236\t    constat_fdr_controlled_rate: 0.31\n237\t    constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.19\n238\t    constat_enrichment_vs_li: 1.82\n239\t    constat_fpr_on_clean: 0.08\n240\t    paraphrase_semantic_similarity: 0.93\n241\t    paraphrase_quality_human_rating: 4.2\n242\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, ConStat performs respectably on non-RL models\n243\t    (detection rate 0.44) but degrades sharply on RL models (0.12), confirming the\n244\t    complementary detection hypothesis. The key finding for the paper narrative is\n245\t    the low Jaccard overlap (0.19) between ConStat and DIF flags — these methods identify\n246\t    fundamentally different item properties. ConStat''s higher enrichment against\n247\t    Li et al. labels (1.82 vs DIF''s 0.96) actually supports the meta-critique: ConStat\n248\t    detects items with web exposure that also show perturbation sensitivity (a subset\n249\t    of globally contaminated items), while DIF captures temporal differential functioning\n250\t    regardless of web exposure. This directly illustrates why web-overlap ground truth\n251\t    works for ConStat-type detectors but not for DIF-type detectors. Reviewers see\n252\t    this as strong empirical support for the claim that different detectors measure\n253\t    different constructs.\n254\t\n255\t    '\n256\t  tables:\n257\t  - name: 'Table: ConStat Detection Power by Model Type'\n258\t    columns:\n259\t    - Model type\n260\t    - N_models\n261\t    - Detection rate\n262\t    - FDR-corrected\n263\t    rows:\n264\t    - - SFT-only\n265\t      - 50\n266\t      - '0.44'\n267\t      - '0.37'\n268\t    - - Alignment-RL\n269\t      - 50\n270\t      - '0.31'\n271\t      - '0.24'\n272\t    - - Reasoning-RL\n273\t      - 13\n274\t      - '0.12'\n275\t      - '0.07'\n276\t    - - All\n277\t      - 113\n278\t      - '0.38'\n279\t      - '0.31'\n280\t  figures_description:\n281\t  - 'Figure: Venn diagram of DIF-C flags vs ConStat flags vs Li et al. contaminated\n282\t    labels on the 247 evaluated items, showing three largely non-overlapping circles\n283\t    with Jaccard(DIF,ConStat)=0.19, Jaccard(DIF,Li)=0.21, Jaccard(ConStat,Li)=0.34.'\n284\t  analysis: 'ConStat results serve the paper as a baseline comparison method. Its\n285\t    strengths (higher Li et al. enrichment) and weaknesses (RL degradation, narrow\n286\t    coverage) are both narratively useful. In the best tier, this experiment is reported\n287\t    in a compressed comparison table rather than as a standalone section, reinforcing\n288\t    Claim 2''s meta-critique without bloating the page budget. Moved to appendix in\n289\t    the 8-page version.\n290\t\n291\t    '\n292\t- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n293\t  plan_id: plan_003b\n294\t  description: 'Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU. 10-15 open-weight\n295\t    models, 50 responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection.\n296\t\n297\t    '\n298\t  expected_results:\n299\t    n_models_evaluated: 12\n300\t    n_items_scored: 12508\n301\t    dvd_detection_rate_overall: 0.22\n302\t    dvd_detection_rate_variant_contamination: 0.58\n303\t    dvd_detection_rate_verbatim_contamination: 0.09\n304\t    dvd_bimodal_pct: 14.3\n305\t    dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.11\n306\t    dvd_enrichment_vs_li: 1.41\n307\t    generation_variance_mean_clean: 0.34\n308\t    generation_variance_mean_contaminated: 0.18\n309\t    temperature_sensitivity: T=0.7 optimal, T=1.0 noisy\n310\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, DVD shows a distinctive detection profile:\n311\t    strong on variant contamination (items memorized in paraphrased form, high variance\n312\t    across generations) but weak on verbatim contamination (where models produce near-deterministic\n313\t    correct answers with low variance). This creates a natural complementarity with\n314\t    DIF, which detects differential functioning regardless of contamination mechanism.\n315\t    The very low Jaccard overlap (0.11) between DVD and DIF flags again reinforces\n316\t    the meta-critique: each detector operates on a different signal, so no single\n317\t    ground truth can validate all approaches. The 14.3% bimodal distribution finding\n318\t    suggests a meaningful fraction of items have split response profiles across model\n319\t    generations — consistent with DIF''s temporal instability finding but measuring\n320\t    a different facet. Reviewers accept this as evidence that the contamination detection\n321\t    space is fragmented, motivating the unified validation framework (Claim 3).\n322\t\n323\t    '\n324\t  tables:\n325\t  - name: 'Table: DVD Detection Power by Contamination Type'\n326\t    columns:\n327\t    - Contamination type\n328\t    - Detection rate\n329\t    - Generation variance (mean)\n330\t    rows:\n331\t    - - Verbatim\n332\t      - '0.09'\n333\t      - '0.07'\n334\t    - - Variant (paraphrased)\n335\t      - '0.58'\n336\t      - '0.41'\n337\t    - - Clean (no contamination)\n338\t      - '0.04'\n339\t      - '0.34'\n340\t  figures_description:\n341\t  - 'Figure: Distribution of generation variance scores for DVD-flagged vs unflagged\n342\t    items, showing bimodal split in flagged items.'\n343\t  analysis: 'DVD complements both DIF and ConStat by targeting a contamination mechanism\n344\t    neither captures well. In the best tier, this is reported as supplementary evidence\n345\t    for the fragmentation thesis. Like plan_003a, compressed to appendix in the 8-page\n346\t    version. The key takeaway for reviewers is that three independent detectors with\n347\t    Jaccard<0.20 pairwise overlap proves the field needs method-specific validation,\n348\t    not one-size-fits-all ground truth.\n349\t\n350\t    '\n351\t- name: ensemble_fusion\n352\t  plan_id: plan_003c\n353\t  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Calibrated thresholds,\n354\t    majority voting, weighted fusion, cross-validated weights.\n355\t\n356\t    '\n357\t  expected_results:\n358\t    ensemble_2of3_coverage: 0.71\n359\t    ensemble_weighted_coverage: 0.76\n360\t    ensemble_fpr_2of3: 0.062\n361\t    ensemble_fpr_weighted: 0.058\n362\t    ensemble_auroc: 0.89\n363\t    dif_solo_auroc: 0.91\n364\t    constat_solo_auroc: 0.72\n365\t    dvd_solo_auroc: 0.64\n366\t    cv_weight_dif: 0.52\n367\t    cv_weight_constat: 0.31\n368\t    cv_weight_dvd: 0.17\n369\t    uniform_vs_optimized_auroc_diff: 0.02\n370\t    coverage_by_regime:\n371\t      large_effect_dif_dominant: δ≥1.4 → DIF TPR=0.76, ensemble=0.78\n372\t      medium_effect_constat_adds: 0.6≤δ<1.4 → DIF TPR=0.31, ensemble=0.54\n373\t      variant_dvd_adds: variant only → DIF TPR=0.08, ensemble=0.42\n374\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the ensemble shows that DIF alone captures\n375\t    most of the large-effect detection power (AUROC 0.91 vs ensemble 0.89 — ensemble\n376\t    slightly lower due to false-positive leakage from weaker signals), but the ensemble\n377\t    provides superior coverage in the medium-effect and variant-contamination regimes\n378\t    where DIF''s power drops below the operational threshold. The cross-validated\n379\t    weights heavily favor DIF (0.52) reflecting its stronger signal-to-noise ratio,\n380\t    with ConStat contributing in the SFT regime and DVD in the variant regime. The\n381\t    key finding is that uniform-weight baseline performs within 0.02 AUROC of optimized\n382\t    weights — the ensemble is robust to weight miscalibration. FPR<0.10 across all\n383\t    configurations satisfies the precision constraint. However, in the best tier this\n384\t    is NOT in the main paper — it''s appendix material. The narrative has pivoted\n385\t    from \"DIF detects contamination\" to \"DIF characterizes benchmarks,\" making ensemble\n386\t    detection a secondary contribution. Reviewers appreciate the thoroughness but\n387\t    don''t require it for acceptance.\n388\t\n389\t    '\n390\t  tables:\n391\t  - name: 'Table: Ensemble vs Individual Detector Performance'\n392\t    columns:\n393\t    - Method\n394\t    - AUROC\n395\t    - Coverage@FPR<0.10\n396\t    - Regime strength\n397\t    rows:\n398\t    - - DIF (MH)\n399\t      - '0.91'\n400\t      - '0.68'\n401\t      - Large-effect differential\n402\t    - - ConStat\n403\t      - '0.72'\n404\t      - '0.44'\n405\t      - Medium-effect, non-RL\n406\t    - - DVD\n407\t      - '0.64'\n408\t      - '0.31'\n409\t      - Variant contamination\n410\t    - - Ensemble (2-of-3)\n411\t      - '0.89'\n412\t      - '0.71'\n413\t      - Broad coverage\n414\t    - - Ensemble (weighted)\n415\t      - '0.89'\n416\t      - '0.76'\n417\t      - Broad coverage\n418\t  figures_description:\n419\t  - 'Figure: ROC curves for individual detectors and ensemble, showing DIF dominating\n420\t    at low FPR but ensemble superior at moderate FPR thresholds.'\n421\t  analysis: 'Ensemble fusion demonstrates the complementarity thesis empirically but\n422\t    is not load-bearing for any of the 4 claims. In the best tier, it appears in the\n423\t    appendix as \"future direction validated in preliminary form.\" The main paper mentions\n424\t    it in one sentence in Discussion: \"preliminary ensemble fusion (Appendix X) suggests\n425\t    complementary coverage across detection regimes.\" This keeps the paper focused\n426\t    on characterization rather than detection.\n427\t\n428\t    '\n429\t- name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n430\t  plan_id: plan_004\n431\t  description: 'Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (METABENCH, 1,319 items × 6,702 models)\n432\t    with GSM1K contamination gap as external validation. Three steps: temporal DIF,\n433\t    semi-synthetic validation, and GSM1K correlation.\n434\t\n435\t    '\n436\t  expected_results:\n437\t    step1_gsm8k_temporal_dif:\n438\t      n_items: 1319\n439\t      n_ref: 1017\n440\t      n_foc: 804\n441\t      standard_c_pct: 18.9\n442\t      external_c_pct: 47.4\n443\t      sanity_c_pct: 0.3\n444\t      standard_external_spearman: 0.926\n445\t    step2_semi_synthetic:\n446\t      standard_delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n447\t      standard_delta_tpr_p10: 0.818\n448\t      external_delta_tpr_p10: 0.517\n449\t      fpr_stable: true\n450\t    step3_gsm1k_correlation:\n451\t      n_models: 33\n452\t      pearson_r: -0.478\n453\t      pearson_p: 0.005\n454\t      pearson_bca_ci: '[-0.714, -0.155]'\n455\t      spearman_rho: -0.403\n456\t      spearman_p: 0.02\n457\t      spearman_bca_ci: '[-0.687, -0.031]'\n458\t      partial_r_controlling_accuracy: -0.385\n459\t      partial_p: 0.03\n460\t      partial_bca_ci: '[-0.640, -0.058]'\n461\t      permutation_p: 0.014\n462\t      leave_one_out_r_range: '[-0.537, -0.374]'\n463\t      cook_d_max_model: Deepseek-math-7b-rl\n464\t      robust: true\n465\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the GSM8K replication serves three purposes.\n466\t    Step 1: GSM8K''s lower C% (18.9% vs MMLU''s 31.3%) is expected — GSM8K is a single-domain\n467\t    math benchmark with less construct heterogeneity, so fewer items exhibit differential\n468\t    functioning. This is actually GOOD — it shows DIF rates are domain-appropriate,\n469\t    not inflated by methodology. Step 2: ΔTPR=+0.818 (standard matching) on GSM8K\n470\t    exceeds MMLU''s +0.674, confirming the semi-synthetic framework is portable and\n471\t    more powerful on single-domain benchmarks where matching is cleaner. Step 3: The\n472\t    anti-correlation r=-0.478 (p=0.005) between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination\n473\t    gap is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence. Models with larger contamination\n474\t    gaps (GSM8K >> GSM1K) show disproportionately LOWER performance on DIF-C items\n475\t    — consistent with contamination producing surface gains on non-DIF items while\n476\t    DIF items capture genuine capability. All CIs exclude zero; leave-one-out stability\n477\t    (r range [-0.537, -0.374]) and Cook''s D analysis confirm no single influential\n478\t    model drives the result. In the best tier, reviewers accept N=33 as suggestive\n479\t    corroboration (not a standalone proof) because all statistical tests converge\n480\t    and the effect direction is theoretically coherent. The wide CI upper bounds (Pearson\n481\t    |r|=0.155, Spearman |ρ|=0.031) are honestly reported in limitations, and reviewers\n482\t    appreciate the transparency rather than attacking it.\n483\t\n484\t    '\n485\t  tables:\n486\t  - name: 'Table: GSM1K Cross-Benchmark Validation'\n487\t    columns:\n488\t    - Statistic\n489\t    - Estimate\n490\t    - p-value\n491\t    - 95% BCa CI\n492\t    rows:\n493\t    - - Pearson r\n494\t      - '-0.478'\n495\t      - '0.005'\n496\t      - '[-0.714, -0.155]'\n497\t    - - Spearman ρ\n498\t      - '-0.403'\n499\t      - '0.020'\n500\t      - '[-0.687, -0.031]'\n501\t    - - Partial r (ctrl acc)\n502\t      - '-0.385'\n503\t      - '0.030'\n504\t      - '[-0.640, -0.058]'\n505\t    - - Permutation p\n506\t      - —\n507\t      - '0.014'\n508\t      - —\n509\t  figures_description:\n510\t  - 'Figure: Scatter plot of per-model DIF-C item accuracy vs GSM8K−GSM1K contamination\n511\t    gap (N=33 models), showing negative association with regression line and 95% CI\n512\t    band.'\n513\t  - 'Figure: Dose-response curve for GSM8K semi-synthetic injection, showing ΔTPR\n514\t    climbing monotonically from p=0.3 to p=1.0.'\n515\t  analysis: 'GSM8K replication is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence in the paper.\n516\t    In the best tier, the Step 3 correlation survives scrutiny because: (1) all four\n517\t    statistical tests (Pearson, Spearman, partial, permutation) converge on significance,\n518\t    (2) effect direction is theoretically predicted (DIF items resist contamination-based\n519\t    gains), (3) leave-one-out shows robustness. The honest reporting of wide CIs and\n520\t    N=33 limitation actually builds reviewer trust — overclaiming would trigger more\n521\t    skepticism than underclaiming.\n522\t\n523\t    '\n524\t- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n525\t  plan_id: plan_005\n526\t  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU. Fit bifactor model (general +\n527\t    57 subject-specific factors), select anchor items, fixed-parameter calibration\n528\t    of replacements, demonstrate equated score equivalence.\n529\t\n530\t    '\n531\t  expected_results:\n532\t    bifactor_rmsea: 0.028\n533\t    bifactor_cfi: 0.91\n534\t    bifactor_tli: 0.89\n535\t    bifactor_srmr: 0.042\n536\t    general_factor_variance_explained: 0.34\n537\t    subject_factor_variance_explained: 0.41\n538\t    n_anchor_items_selected: 4200\n539\t    anchor_dif_free_pct: 100\n540\t    equated_rho_original_vs_replaced: 0.964\n541\t    equated_tau_original_vs_replaced: 0.951\n542\t    calibration_convergence_rate: 0.92\n543\t    mean_equating_error_theta: 0.08\n544\t    max_equating_error_theta: 0.31\n545\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the bifactor model achieves acceptable (not\n546\t    excellent) fit — RMSEA=0.028 is good, but CFI=0.91 and TLI=0.89 are at the conventional\n547\t    threshold, reflecting MMLU''s inherent 57-domain multidimensionality that no simple\n548\t    factor structure fully captures. The general factor explains only 34% of variance,\n549\t    with subject-specific factors at 41% — consistent with MMLU being more of a test\n550\t    battery than a unidimensional measure. This is itself an important finding: it\n551\t    empirically quantifies why unidimensional IRT is misspecified for MMLU (explaining\n552\t    the |Lz|>2 misfit observed in 98% of models). The equated rankings achieve ρ=0.964\n553\t    (above the ≥0.95 target), demonstrating that psychometric equating is feasible\n554\t    even with imperfect model fit. In the best tier, this pilot is reported in 2-3\n555\t    sentences in Discussion as \"bifactor equating is feasible; full development is\n556\t    future work\" rather than as a standalone claim. The marginal fit metrics are honestly\n557\t    reported and positioned as motivation for multidimensional IRT extensions.\n558\t\n559\t    '\n560\t  tables:\n561\t  - name: 'Table: Bifactor IRT Fit Indices'\n562\t    columns:\n563\t    - Index\n564\t    - Value\n565\t    - Threshold\n566\t    - Verdict\n567\t    rows:\n568\t    - - RMSEA\n569\t      - '0.028'\n570\t      - <0.05\n571\t      - Good\n572\t    - - CFI\n573\t      - '0.91'\n574\t      - '>0.95'\n575\t      - Marginal\n576\t    - - TLI\n577\t      - '0.89'\n578\t      - '>0.95'\n579\t      - Marginal\n580\t    - - SRMR\n581\t      - '0.042'\n582\t      - <0.08\n583\t      - Good\n584\t  figures_description:\n585\t  - 'Figure: Scatter plot of equated θ (bifactor-anchored) vs original θ (raw total\n586\t    score) for 5,219 models, showing tight correlation with ρ=0.964.'\n587\t  analysis: 'Bifactor results serve as proof-of-concept for the equating pipeline,\n588\t    not as a polished contribution. In the best tier, the marginal CFI/TLI values\n589\t    are a feature, not a bug — they empirically demonstrate that MMLU requires multidimensional\n590\t    treatment, supporting the limitations section''s call for future MIRT work. The\n591\t    successful equating despite imperfect fit shows the approach is promising enough\n592\t    to warrant dedicated follow-up.\n593\t\n594\t    '\n595\t- name: item_generation_calibration\n596\t  plan_id: plan_006\n597\t  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM\n598\t    generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation, validate\n599\t    difficulty correlation.\n600\t\n601\t    '\n602\t  expected_results:\n603\t    n_items_generated: 500\n604\t    n_dif_flagged_targeted: 200\n605\t    generation_quality_human_rating: 3.8\n606\t    difficulty_corr_prompt_based_rho: 0.32\n607\t    difficulty_corr_student_sim_rho: 0.71\n608\t    difficulty_corr_empirical_rho: 0.68\n609\t    construct_alignment_pct: 0.84\n610\t    cognitive_level_match_pct: 0.78\n611\t    holdout_panel_rho: 0.65\n612\t    generation_reject_rate: 0.22\n613\t    avg_generation_attempts: 1.8\n614\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, LLM-generated replacement items achieve reasonable\n615\t    but imperfect quality. The key finding is the gap between prompt-based difficulty\n616\t    targeting (ρ=0.32, consistent with literature''s 0.10-0.45) and student simulation\n617\t    calibration (ρ=0.71) — simulation doubles the difficulty prediction accuracy,\n618\t    validating the calibration approach. The empirical correlation (ρ=0.68) slightly\n619\t    lags simulation prediction, reflecting real-world noise that simulation doesn''t\n620\t    fully capture. Construct alignment (84%) and cognitive level matching (78%) are\n621\t    adequate for a pilot but below production quality, honestly reported. In the best\n622\t    tier, this appears as 1-2 sentences in Future Work: \"preliminary item generation\n623\t    with student simulation calibration achieves ρ=0.71 difficulty correlation, suggesting\n624\t    automated benchmark maintenance is feasible.\" Reviewers don''t evaluate this as\n625\t    a standalone contribution — it''s a forward-looking demonstration.\n626\t\n627\t    '\n628\t  tables:\n629\t  - name: 'Table: Item Difficulty Prediction Accuracy'\n630\t    columns:\n631\t    - Method\n632\t    - ρ with empirical difficulty\n633\t    - 95% CI\n634\t    rows:\n635\t    - - Prompt-based targeting\n636\t      - '0.32'\n637\t      - '[0.19, 0.44]'\n638\t    - - Student simulation\n639\t      - '0.71'\n640\t      - '[0.64, 0.77]'\n641\t    - - Empirical (holdout panel)\n642\t      - '0.68'\n643\t      - '[0.60, 0.75]'\n644\t  figures_description:\n645\t  - 'Figure: Calibration plot of simulated vs empirical item difficulty for 200 generated\n646\t    replacement items, with ρ=0.71 and 45-degree reference line.'\n647\t  analysis: 'Item generation is explicitly positioned as future work in the best tier.\n648\t    The ρ=0.71 result is promising enough to motivate follow-up but not strong enough\n649\t    to claim as a contribution. In the EMNLP submission, this is at most an appendix\n650\t    entry demonstrating the practical viability of the DIF-informed benchmark maintenance\n651\t    pipeline.\n652\t\n653\t    '\n654\t- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n655\t  plan_id: plan_007\n656\t  description: 'Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) over\n657\t    4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power accumulation for medium effects.\n658\t\n659\t    '\n660\t  expected_results:\n661\t    admin_1_power_d10: 0.38\n662\t    admin_2_power_d10: 0.54\n663\t    admin_3_power_d10: 0.69\n664\t    admin_4_power_d10: 0.78\n665\t    admin_1_power_d14: 0.68\n666\t    admin_2_power_d14: 0.82\n667\t    admin_3_power_d14: 0.91\n668\t    admin_4_power_d14: 0.95\n669\t    cumulative_fpr_admin4: 0.048\n670\t    bayes_factor_threshold: 3.0\n671\t    prior_update_mechanism: posterior from admin t → prior for admin t+1\n672\t    simulation_n_items: 1000\n673\t    simulation_n_models_per_admin: 200\n674\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the cumulative Bayesian approach shows clean\n675\t    power accumulation: from 0.38 (single administration, δ=1.0) to 0.69 after 3 administrations,\n676\t    crossing the 0.70 power threshold. At δ=1.4 (the operational threshold identified\n677\t    in power simulations), power reaches 0.91 by administration 3 and 0.95 by administration\n678\t    4. The FPR remains controlled at 0.048 (below 0.05) even after 4 accumulation\n679\t    rounds, confirming the Bayesian framework properly regularizes false positives.\n680\t    This is a simulation-only result — no real multi-wave longitudinal data exists\n681\t    for LLM benchmarks. In the best tier, this is purely appendix material: \"Simulation\n682\t    shows cumulative Bayesian DIF achieves 0.69 power at δ=1.0 after 3 administrations,\n683\t    suggesting longitudinal benchmark monitoring is feasible.\" Reviewers see it as\n684\t    nice-to-have future work, not a current contribution.\n685\t\n686\t    '\n687\t  tables:\n688\t  - name: 'Table: Cumulative Power by Administration Wave'\n689\t    columns:\n690\t    - Administration\n691\t    - Power (δ=1.0)\n692\t    - Power (δ=1.4)\n693\t    - FPR\n694\t    rows:\n695\t    - - '1'\n696\t      - '0.38'\n697\t      - '0.68'\n698\t      - '0.042'\n699\t    - - '2'\n700\t      - '0.54'\n701\t      - '0.82'\n702\t      - '0.044'\n703\t    - - '3'\n704\t      - '0.69'\n705\t      - '0.91'\n706\t      - '0.046'\n707\t    - - '4'\n708\t      - '0.78'\n709\t      - '0.95'\n710\t      - '0.048'\n711\t  figures_description:\n712\t  - 'Figure: Power curves across 4 administrations for δ∈{0.6, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4,\n713\t    1.6}, showing monotonic accumulation with diminishing returns after administration\n714\t    3.'\n715\t  analysis: 'Cumulative Bayesian DIF is the most forward-looking experiment in the\n716\t    plan. In the best tier, it provides the theoretical foundation for the Conclusion''s\n717\t    recommendation that \"benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits periodically\"\n718\t    — by showing that cumulative monitoring outperforms repeated one-shot testing.\n719\t    Entirely simulation-based and relegated to appendix, but it gives the paper a\n720\t    constructive vision beyond diagnosis.\n721\t\n722\t    '\n723\t- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n724\t  plan_id: plan_008\n725\t  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.\n726\t    Simulate 5-50% replacement rates, measure ranking stability, reliability, coverage.\n727\t\n728\t    '\n729\t  expected_results:\n730\t    replacement_5pct_rho: 0.999\n731\t    replacement_10pct_rho: 0.998\n732\t    replacement_20pct_rho: 0.994\n733\t    replacement_50pct_rho: 0.976\n734\t    reliability_shr_original: 0.993\n735\t    reliability_shr_5pct: 0.992\n736\t    reliability_shr_20pct: 0.987\n737\t    reliability_shr_50pct: 0.969\n738\t    construct_coverage_original: 1.0\n739\t    construct_coverage_20pct: 0.96\n740\t    construct_coverage_50pct: 0.88\n741\t    theoretical_bound_rho_lower: ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (r = replacement fraction)\n742\t    critical_replacement_threshold: 0.35\n743\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, graceful degradation shows that MMLU''s massive\n744\t    redundancy (already revealed by plan_017''s ρ≈0.997 after 31% DIF removal) translates\n745\t    into a wide safe zone for item replacement. Up to 20% replacement, ranking stability\n746\t    stays above 0.99 and reliability above 0.98. The critical threshold at 35% marks\n747\t    where construct coverage drops below 0.95 — interestingly close to the 31% DIF\n748\t    rate, suggesting that replacing all DIF items simultaneously would be at the edge\n749\t    of the safe zone. This is a useful calibration result: benchmark maintainers can\n750\t    replace DIF items in waves (10-15% per refresh) without compromising measurement\n751\t    properties. The theoretical bound ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (where r = replacement fraction)\n752\t    provides a practical planning tool. In the best tier, this appears as 2-3 sentences\n753\t    in Discussion supporting the recommendation that DIF-informed benchmark refresh\n754\t    is operationally safe.\n755\t\n756\t    '\n757\t  tables:\n758\t  - name: 'Table: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement'\n759\t    columns:\n760\t    - Replacement %\n761\t    - ρ (ranking)\n762\t    - SHR (reliability)\n763\t    - Coverage\n764\t    rows:\n765\t    - - 0% (baseline)\n766\t      - '1.000'\n767\t      - '0.993'\n768\t      - '1.00'\n769\t    - - 5%\n770\t      - '0.999'\n771\t      - '0.992'\n772\t      - '0.99'\n773\t    - - 10%\n774\t      - '0.998'\n775\t      - '0.990'\n776\t      - '0.98'\n777\t    - - 20%\n778\t      - '0.994'\n779\t      - '0.987'\n780\t      - '0.96'\n781\t    - - 31% (all DIF)\n782\t      - '0.989'\n783\t      - '0.982'\n784\t      - '0.93'\n785\t    - - 50%\n786\t      - '0.976'\n787\t      - '0.969'\n788\t      - '0.88'\n789\t  figures_description:\n790\t  - 'Figure: Three-panel degradation curves (ρ, SHR, coverage) as function of replacement\n791\t    fraction, with vertical line at 31% (DIF rate) and shaded safe zone.'\n792\t  analysis: 'Graceful degradation complements plan_017''s ranking stability finding\n793\t    by extending it to the replacement scenario. In the best tier, this empirically\n794\t    grounds the practical recommendation in the Conclusion. The 35% critical threshold\n795\t    being near the 31% DIF rate creates a natural narrative: \"the current level of\n796\t    instability is at the edge of MMLU''s redundancy buffer — one more model generation\n797\t    may exceed it.\" This urgency framing, backed by quantitative bounds, strengthens\n798\t    the paper''s call to action without being alarmist.\n799\t\n800\t    '\n801\t- name: item_focused_trees\n802\t  plan_id: plan_009\n803\t  description: 'Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative\n804\t    to categorical cohort DIF. Model release date as continuous covariate, compare\n805\t    with MH-DIF.\n806\t\n807\t    '\n808\t  expected_results:\n809\t    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n810\t    ift_significant_temporal_pct: 34.7\n811\t    ift_vs_mh_c_jaccard: 0.72\n812\t    ift_mh_concordance_rate: 0.81\n813\t    ift_additional_detections: 423\n814\t    ift_missed_by_mh: 0\n815\t    ift_optimal_split_date: '2023-09-15'\n816\t    ift_nonlinear_items_pct: 8.3\n817\t    ift_computation_time_hours: 2.4\n818\t    ift_vs_mh_rank_corr_delta_mh: 0.88\n819\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, Item-Focused Trees confirm the MH-DIF findings\n820\t    while adding nuance. The 34.7% IFT-significant rate slightly exceeds MH''s 31.3%\n821\t    because IFT captures gradual temporal trends that MH''s binary split misses, plus\n822\t    8.3% of items show nonlinear temporal patterns (e.g., stability→instability→restabilization).\n823\t    The high concordance (Jaccard=0.72, concordance=0.81) validates MH''s simpler\n824\t    approach for the primary analysis, while IFT''s additional detections (423 items)\n825\t    represent borderline cases near MH''s threshold. The optimal split date (2023-09-15)\n826\t    aligns with the ChatGPT-era capability jump, providing temporal specificity that\n827\t    MH''s cohort-level analysis cannot. Zero missed detections (IFT ⊃ MH-C) confirms\n828\t    MH is conservative, supporting the \"31% is a lower bound\" framing. In the best\n829\t    tier, IFT appears as a robustness check in appendix: \"continuous-covariate IFT\n830\t    analysis confirms the temporal DIF pattern (concordance=0.81) and identifies the\n831\t    optimal split near 2023-09, consistent with known capability transitions.\"\n832\t\n833\t    '\n834\t  tables:\n835\t  - name: 'Table: IFT vs MH-DIF Concordance'\n836\t    columns:\n837\t    - Category\n838\t    - MH-C items\n839\t    - IFT-significant items\n840\t    - Overlap\n841\t    rows:\n842\t    - - Both flagged\n843\t      - —\n844\t      - —\n845\t      - 3495\n846\t    - - MH-C only\n847\t      - —\n848\t      - —\n849\t      - 0\n850\t    - - IFT only\n851\t      - —\n852\t      - —\n853\t      - 423\n854\t    - - Neither\n855\t      - —\n856\t      - —\n857\t      - 8590\n858\t    - - Concordance\n859\t      - —\n860\t      - —\n861\t      - '0.81'\n862\t  figures_description:\n863\t  - 'Figure: Item-level temporal DIF trajectory for 3 exemplar items, showing IFT''s\n864\t    continuous covariate function vs MH''s binary flag, with release date on x-axis.'\n865\t  analysis: 'IFT provides methodological depth and confirms the MH analysis is appropriate.\n866\t    In the best tier, the zero-miss result (MH-C ⊂ IFT) is particularly valuable —\n867\t    it means MH''s simpler approach doesn''t miss important signals, just captures\n868\t    fewer borderline cases. This defuses any reviewer concern about arbitrary cohort\n869\t    splitting. Relegated to appendix as robustness check.\n870\t\n871\t    '\n872\t- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n873\t  plan_id: plan_010\n874\t  description: 'Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations. Subset to\n875\t    decoder-only models, compare IRT fit and DIF detection vs full population.\n876\t\n877\t    '\n878\t  expected_results:\n879\t    n_decoder_only: 5226\n880\t    n_non_decoder: 1\n881\t    decoder_only_pct: 99.98\n882\t    c_pct_decoder_only: 31.3\n883\t    c_pct_full: 31.3\n884\t    jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full: 1.0\n885\t    delta_mh_rank_corr: 0.9999\n886\t    irt_rmsea_full: 0.089\n887\t    irt_rmsea_decoder_only: 0.088\n888\t    irt_lz_pct_above_2_full: 98.1\n889\t    irt_lz_pct_above_2_decoder_only: 98.0\n890\t    removed_model: RWKV-based (1 model)\n891\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the architecture stratification result is maximally\n892\t    clean: removing the single non-decoder-only model (RWKV) changes absolutely nothing\n893\t    (Jaccard=1.0, identical C%). This is because METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only\n894\t    — architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue by construction. The IRT fit metrics\n895\t    barely change because the |Lz|>2 misfit reflects MMLU''s 57-domain multidimensionality,\n896\t    not architecture mixing. This result preempts the \"spurious DIF from architecture\n897\t    heterogeneity\" concern in a single sentence: \"METABENCH MMLU contains 5,226/5,227\n898\t    decoder-only models; excluding the 1 non-decoder model yields identical DIF flags\n899\t    (Jaccard=1.0).\" In the best tier, reviewers see this preemptive control as a sign\n900\t    of thoroughness. The persistent |Lz|>2 is discussed in limitations as inherent\n901\t    to applying 1D IRT to a multidimensional test battery — not a methodological failure\n902\t    but a known constraint motivating future MIRT work.\n903\t\n904\t    '\n905\t  tables:\n906\t  - name: 'Table: Architecture Stratification Impact'\n907\t    columns:\n908\t    - Population\n909\t    - N\n910\t    - C%\n911\t    - Jaccard vs full\n912\t    - RMSEA\n913\t    rows:\n914\t    - - Full (all architectures)\n915\t      - 5227\n916\t      - '31.3'\n917\t      - —\n918\t      - '0.089'\n919\t    - - Decoder-only\n920\t      - 5226\n921\t      - '31.3'\n922\t      - '1.0'\n923\t      - '0.088'\n924\t  figures_description:\n925\t  - 'Figure: ΔMH scatter plot (full vs decoder-only) for 12,508 items — perfect correlation\n926\t    with all points on the identity line.'\n927\t  analysis: 'Architecture stratification is the simplest experiment in the plan but\n928\t    one of the most valuable rhetorically. In the best tier, the Jaccard=1.0 result\n929\t    is a one-line knockout of a potential reviewer concern. It also reveals an important\n930\t    meta-insight: the METABENCH MMLU ecosystem is architecturally homogeneous, so\n931\t    any observed DIF must reflect temporal/training differences, not architectural\n932\t    ones. This eliminates an entire class of confounds.\n933\t\n934\t    '\n935\t- name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n936\t  plan_id: plan_011\n937\t  description: 'Inject known contamination into real METABENCH response matrix. Controlled\n938\t    ground truth for DIF detection power. Vary injection prevalence, exploitation\n939\t    rate, focal group size.\n940\t\n941\t    '\n942\t  expected_results:\n943\t    temporal_cohort_results:\n944\t      baseline_fpr: 0.314\n945\t      tpr_p00: 0.326\n946\t      tpr_p03: 0.548\n947\t      tpr_p05: 0.834\n948\t      tpr_p07: 0.99\n949\t      tpr_p10: 1.0\n950\t      delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n951\t      delta_tpr_p10: 0.674\n952\t      fpr_inflation_at_p10: 0.302\n953\t    ability_cohort_results:\n954\t      baseline_fpr: 0.493\n955\t      tpr_p00: 0.498\n956\t      tpr_p10: 0.8\n957\t      delta_tpr_p10: 0.302\n958\t    external_matching_results:\n959\t      fpr: 0.314\n960\t      tpr_p05: 0.834\n961\t      tpr_p10: 1.0\n962\t      fpr_stable_across_injection: true\n963\t    cross_subject_matching_results:\n964\t      delta_fpr_all_injection_levels: 0.0\n965\t      tpr_improvement_over_within: 4/5 subjects improved\n966\t    dose_response_clean: true\n967\t    exploitation_rate_sensitivity:\n968\t      p04_tpr: 0.62\n969\t      p06_tpr: 0.91\n970\t      p08_tpr: 0.98\n971\t  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the semi-synthetic validation provides the\n972\t    controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. The ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates\n973\t    substantial detection power: injecting 50% exploitation rate into 200 items produces\n974\t    a +0.508 increase in true positive rate above the temporal baseline. The dose-response\n975\t    curve is clean and monotonic — from p=0.3 (ΔTPR≈0.22) through p=0.5 (+0.508) to\n976\t    p=1.0 (+0.674) — confirming that DIF detection power scales predictably with contamination\n977\t    intensity. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all injection\n978\t    levels) is the methodological contribution: it eliminates the FPR inflation that\n979\t    plagues within-subject matching (which inflates FPR by +0.186 because the matching\n980\t    variable itself becomes contaminated). This finding has practical value — any\n981\t    future DIF application to multi-domain benchmarks should use cross-subject matching.\n982\t    The ability cohort shows weaker ΔTPR (+0.302) because the baseline FPR is already\n983\t    high (0.493), leaving less room for improvement. In the best tier, the bug fix\n984\t    (inf-delta classified as extreme DIF not class A) is transparently reported as\n985\t    a methodological note, increasing reviewer trust. The matching variable contamination\n986\t    finding is recognized as a subtle and important methodological insight.\n987\t\n988\t    '\n989\t  tables:\n990\t  - name: 'Table: Semi-Synthetic Validation — Dose-Response'\n991\t    columns:\n992\t    - Exploitation rate (p)\n993\t    - TPR (temporal)\n994\t    - ΔTPR\n995\t    - FPR (external match)\n996\t    rows:\n997\t    - - 0.0 (baseline)\n998\t      - '0.326'\n999\t      - —\n1000\t      - '0.314'\n1001\t    - - '0.3'\n1002\t      - '0.548'\n1003\t      - '+0.222'\n1004\t      - '0.314'\n1005\t    - - '0.5'\n1006\t      - '0.834'\n1007\t      - '+0.508'\n1008\t      - '0.314'\n1009\t    - - '0.7'\n1010\t      - '0.990'\n1011\t      - '+0.664'\n1012\t      - '0.314'\n1013\t    - - '1.0'\n1014\t      - '1.000'\n1015\t      - '+0.674'\n1016\t      - '0.314'\n1017\t  - name: 'Table: Matching Strategy Comparison'\n1018\t    columns:\n1019\t    - Matching strategy\n1020\t    - ΔFPR (p=1.0)\n1021\t    - ΔTPR (p=0.5)\n1022\t    - Verdict\n1023\t    rows:\n1024\t    - - Standard (within-subject)\n1025\t      - '+0.302'\n1026\t      - '+0.508'\n1027\t      - FPR inflated\n1028\t    - - External (cross-subject)\n1029\t      - '0.000'\n1030\t      - '+0.508'\n1031\t      - FPR controlled ✓\n1032\t    - - Cross-benchmark (GSM8K)\n1033\t      - '0.000'\n1034\t      - '+0.818'\n1035\t      - FPR controlled ✓\n1036\t  figures_description:\n1037\t  - 'Figure: Dose-response curves for temporal and ability cohorts, showing ΔTPR as\n1038\t    function of exploitation rate p, with shaded 95% bootstrap CIs.'\n1039\t  - 'Figure: FPR comparison across matching strategies at increasing injection levels,\n1040\t    showing external matching stable at 0.314 while standard matching inflates to\n1041\t    0.616.'\n1042\t  analysis: 'The semi-synthetic framework is the paper''s methodological contribution\n1043\t    to the field — Claim 3 rests entirely on this experiment. In the best tier, the\n1044\t    ΔTPR metric is recognized as a principled way to evaluate any item-level contamination\n1045\t    detector under controlled conditions, and the cross-subject matching insight is\n1046\t    appreciated as a practical recommendation with general applicability. The dose-response\n1047\t    linearity gives practitioners intuitive guidance: \"at 50% exploitation, DIF detects\n1048\t    with ΔTPR≈0.5.\" The FPR=0.314 baseline (reflecting the 31.3% temporal DIF rate)\n1049\t    is not a false positive problem — it''s the true temporal instability rate, which\n1050\t    the injected contamination adds to. Reviewers in the best tier understand this\n1051\t    distinction.'\n1052\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0387b3d5-0b40-4998-a89e-ed7775c5671d","uuid":"faf3d67c-cd4f-4413-8b50-a7ede845426e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:32:39.665Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Original framing fully intact. Lead with ''first large-scale\n  psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability'' — the 31.3% finding, the\n  meta-critique, the validation framework, and the diagnostic lens all stand as stated.\n  No claim softening needed. Paper positions DIF as the missing diagnostic layer the\n  evaluation community didn''t know it needed. All 4 claims survive review intact;\n  ''instability ≠ invalidity'' framing is recognized as a genuine conceptual contribution;\n  meta-critique lands as field-wide insight. Reviewers accept ability confound controls,\n  GSM1K corroboration as suggestive, and model independence controls as preemptive.\n  8-page EMNLP compression retains all 4 claims without feeling rushed. Conclusion\n  makes forward-looking recommendations (benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits\n  periodically) without hedging.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: real_data_dif_mmlu\n  plan_id: plan_001\n  description: 'Apply MH-DIF to full METABENCH MMLU response matrix (5,219 models\n    × 12,508 items). Temporal cohort (2023 vs 2024-H1), ability-quintile cohort, architecture\n    stratification, within-family confound control (Llama-2 vs Llama-3), domain-specificity\n    analysis, and proxy validation against Li et al. web-overlap labels.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    temporal_ets_c_pct: 31.3\n    temporal_enrichment_vs_li: 0.96\n    temporal_fpr_on_li_clean: 0.318\n    ability_quintile_ets_c_pct: 2.9\n    ability_quintile_enrichment: 1.19\n    within_family_llama2v3_c_pct: 61.3\n    within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct: 38.7\n    domain_spearman_rho: 0.307\n    domain_spearman_p: 0.02\n    sanity_random_split_c_pct: 0.18\n    temporal_ability_jaccard: 0.014\n    n_subjects_with_dif: 57\n    subject_c_pct_range: 20.7–48.4\n    subject_c_pct_unweighted_mean: 30.0\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the temporal cohort analysis cleanly reveals\n    31.3% ETS-C items — a number that is striking enough to command attention but\n    not so extreme as to suggest methodological artifact. The near-unity enrichment\n    (0.96) against Li et al. labels is the keystone of the meta-critique: DIF flags\n    are orthogonal to web-overlap labels, confirming that these measure fundamentally\n    different constructs (differential vs global exposure). The ability-quintile cohort\n    shows only 2.9% C-items — a critical internal control proving that when groups\n    differ only in ability (not temporally), DIF correctly produces near-zero flags.\n    The random-split sanity check at 0.18% eliminates statistical artifact concerns\n    entirely. Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is high because these families have\n    maximal temporal separation AND known architecture/training changes — this becomes\n    a positive result showing DIF captures genuine cross-generation functional shifts.\n    Domain ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) is modest but significant, appropriately handled by plan_019\n    decomposition showing domain improvement explains only 2.4pp. All 57 subjects\n    showing some instability (range 20.7–48.4%) demonstrates this is a benchmark-wide\n    phenomenon, not concentrated in a few pathological domains. Reviewers accept this\n    as the definitive large-scale characterization of MMLU temporal stability.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: DIF Detection Summary by Cohort Definition'\n    columns:\n    - Cohort\n    - N_ref\n    - N_foc\n    - ETS_A%\n    - ETS_B%\n    - ETS_C%\n    - Enrichment\n    rows:\n    - - Temporal (2023 vs 2024-H1)\n      - 1253\n      - 656\n      - '47.8'\n      - '20.9'\n      - '31.3'\n      - 0.96\n    - - Ability (Q1 vs Q5)\n      - 1044\n      - 1044\n      - '89.2'\n      - '7.9'\n      - '2.9'\n      - 1.19\n    - - Random split (sanity)\n      - 960\n      - 960\n      - '97.5'\n      - '2.3'\n      - '0.18'\n      - —\n  - name: 'Table 2: Within-Family DIF Confound Control'\n    columns:\n    - Family pair\n    - N_ref\n    - N_foc\n    - C%\n    - Interpretation\n    rows:\n    - - Llama-2 vs Llama-3\n      - 161\n      - 308\n      - '61.3'\n      - Capability gain + architecture shift\n    - - Mistral vs Mixtral\n      - 134\n      - 89\n      - '38.7'\n      - Architecture shift (dense→MoE)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 1: Histogram of per-subject ETS-C percentages across 57 MMLU domains,\n    showing all subjects above 20% with unimodal distribution centered near 30%.'\n  - 'Figure 2: Scatter plot of domain accuracy improvement (Δacc) vs DIF C% with ρ=0.307\n    regression line, showing weak positive association.'\n  analysis: 'The 31.3% figure anchors the entire paper. Its robustness is demonstrated\n    through three internal controls: (1) ability-only cohort produces 2.9% — proving\n    temporal signal is not an ability artifact, (2) random split produces 0.18% —\n    proving it is not statistical noise, (3) enrichment ≈ 1.0 — proving DIF flags\n    are orthogonal to web-overlap contamination labels. The within-family results\n    are higher than temporal (61.3% for Llama-2v3) because these compare families\n    with known architecture changes, validating DIF sensitivity to genuine cross-generation\n    shifts. In the best tier, reviewers see this not as \"DIF fails\" but as \"DIF correctly\n    identifies that MMLU items function differently across model generations.\"\n\n    '\n- name: rl_robustness_validation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 'Validate DIF''s robustness to RL-trained models. SFT-only baseline,\n    alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO) mechanism control, and reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO) focal models.\n    Two-level validation: group-level DIF stability and model-level traditional detector\n    degradation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_sft_baseline: 312\n    n_alignment_rl: 477\n    n_reasoning_rl: 13\n    dif_c_pct_sft_cohort: 30.8\n    dif_c_pct_alignment_rl_cohort: 31.1\n    dif_c_pct_mixed_rl_cohort: 30.5\n    jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.87\n    jaccard_sft_vs_mixed_rl: 0.84\n    constat_auroc_sft: 0.72\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.41\n    mink_auroc_sft: 0.68\n    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.35\n    dif_retention_ratio_vs_constat: 0.97\n    tost_equivalence_p: 0.008\n    bootstrap_ci_dif_auroc: '[0.88, 0.94]'\n    delong_p_dif_vs_constat: 0.003\n    partial_exploitation_boundary_p: 0.45\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, DIF''s group-level nature makes it inherently\n    robust to RL training: MH conditions on total score within the cohort, so individual\n    model training methodology is irrelevant as long as the cohort composition doesn''t\n    systematically shift. With only 13 reasoning-RL models added to a focal group\n    of 656+, the cohort composition barely changes (Jaccard 0.84 with baseline). The\n    C% remains within 1pp of baseline across all three cohort compositions, confirming\n    group-level stability. Meanwhile, ConStat and Min-K% — which operate at the individual\n    model level — degrade sharply on reasoning-RL models (AUROC drops from 0.72/0.68\n    to 0.41/0.35) because GRPO/PPO flatten the NLL landscape these detectors rely\n    on. TOST equivalence testing (p=0.008) formally confirms DIF retention is not\n    just \"failed to reject null\" but positively equivalent. The partial exploitation\n    analysis shows DIF detection boundary at p≈0.45, meaning contamination must affect\n    <45% of focal group to evade — a reasonable operational bound. Reviewers accept\n    the N=13 reasoning-RL sample because DIF''s claim is group-level robustness (adding\n    13 models doesn''t change group statistics), while per-model detector degradation\n    is demonstrated on exactly those 13 models individually.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: DIF Stability Across Cohort Compositions'\n    columns:\n    - Cohort composition\n    - N_focal\n    - C%\n    - Jaccard vs SFT baseline\n    rows:\n    - - SFT-only focal\n      - 312\n      - '30.8'\n      - '1.00'\n    - - + Alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO)\n      - 789\n      - '31.1'\n      - '0.87'\n    - - + Reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\n      - 802\n      - '30.5'\n      - '0.84'\n  - name: 'Table: Per-Model Detector Degradation on Reasoning-RL'\n    columns:\n    - Detector\n    - AUROC (SFT)\n    - AUROC (Reasoning-RL)\n    - Δ\n    - DeLong p\n    rows:\n    - - DIF (group-level)\n      - '0.91'\n      - '0.90'\n      - '-0.01'\n      - '0.82'\n    - - ConStat\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.41'\n      - '-0.31'\n      - '0.003'\n    - - Min-K%\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.35'\n      - '-0.33'\n      - '0.001'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Detection power (AUROC) as function of exploitation rate p∈[0.4,1.0]\n    for DIF, ConStat, and Min-K%. DIF maintains AUROC>0.85 down to p≈0.45; ConStat\n    drops below 0.6 at p=0.7; Min-K% drops below 0.5 at p=0.75.'\n  analysis: 'This experiment serves a dual rhetorical purpose: (1) it shows DIF is\n    robust to the specific threat model (RL concealing contamination signals) that\n    motivates the paper, and (2) it provides a direct head-to-head comparison with\n    existing per-model detectors. In the best tier, the N=13 limitation is acknowledged\n    but reviewers accept the two-level design as sufficient — DIF''s group-level nature\n    means the validation logic is sound even with small RL samples. The partial exploitation\n    analysis adds theoretical depth, characterizing when DIF works rather than just\n    showing that it does.\n\n    '\n- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003a\n  description: 'Independent evaluation of ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+\n    MMLU items. LLM paraphrasing, original vs reformulated comparison, FDR-controlled\n    detection.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_paraphrased: 247\n    constat_detection_rate_all: 0.38\n    constat_detection_rate_sft: 0.44\n    constat_detection_rate_rl: 0.12\n    constat_fdr_controlled_rate: 0.31\n    constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.19\n    constat_enrichment_vs_li: 1.82\n    constat_fpr_on_clean: 0.08\n    paraphrase_semantic_similarity: 0.93\n    paraphrase_quality_human_rating: 4.2\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, ConStat performs respectably on non-RL models\n    (detection rate 0.44) but degrades sharply on RL models (0.12), confirming the\n    complementary detection hypothesis. The key finding for the paper narrative is\n    the low Jaccard overlap (0.19) between ConStat and DIF flags — these methods identify\n    fundamentally different item properties. ConStat''s higher enrichment against\n    Li et al. labels (1.82 vs DIF''s 0.96) actually supports the meta-critique: ConStat\n    detects items with web exposure that also show perturbation sensitivity (a subset\n    of globally contaminated items), while DIF captures temporal differential functioning\n    regardless of web exposure. This directly illustrates why web-overlap ground truth\n    works for ConStat-type detectors but not for DIF-type detectors. Reviewers see\n    this as strong empirical support for the claim that different detectors measure\n    different constructs.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: ConStat Detection Power by Model Type'\n    columns:\n    - Model type\n    - N_models\n    - Detection rate\n    - FDR-corrected\n    rows:\n    - - SFT-only\n      - 50\n      - '0.44'\n      - '0.37'\n    - - Alignment-RL\n      - 50\n      - '0.31'\n      - '0.24'\n    - - Reasoning-RL\n      - 13\n      - '0.12'\n      - '0.07'\n    - - All\n      - 113\n      - '0.38'\n      - '0.31'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Venn diagram of DIF-C flags vs ConStat flags vs Li et al. contaminated\n    labels on the 247 evaluated items, showing three largely non-overlapping circles\n    with Jaccard(DIF,ConStat)=0.19, Jaccard(DIF,Li)=0.21, Jaccard(ConStat,Li)=0.34.'\n  analysis: 'ConStat results serve the paper as a baseline comparison method. Its\n    strengths (higher Li et al. enrichment) and weaknesses (RL degradation, narrow\n    coverage) are both narratively useful. In the best tier, this experiment is reported\n    in a compressed comparison table rather than as a standalone section, reinforcing\n    Claim 2''s meta-critique without bloating the page budget. Moved to appendix in\n    the 8-page version.\n\n    '\n- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003b\n  description: 'Independent evaluation of DVD generation variance on MMLU. 10-15 open-weight\n    models, 50 responses per item via temperature sampling, bimodal distribution detection.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_models_evaluated: 12\n    n_items_scored: 12508\n    dvd_detection_rate_overall: 0.22\n    dvd_detection_rate_variant_contamination: 0.58\n    dvd_detection_rate_verbatim_contamination: 0.09\n    dvd_bimodal_pct: 14.3\n    dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard: 0.11\n    dvd_enrichment_vs_li: 1.41\n    generation_variance_mean_clean: 0.34\n    generation_variance_mean_contaminated: 0.18\n    temperature_sensitivity: T=0.7 optimal, T=1.0 noisy\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, DVD shows a distinctive detection profile:\n    strong on variant contamination (items memorized in paraphrased form, high variance\n    across generations) but weak on verbatim contamination (where models produce near-deterministic\n    correct answers with low variance). This creates a natural complementarity with\n    DIF, which detects differential functioning regardless of contamination mechanism.\n    The very low Jaccard overlap (0.11) between DVD and DIF flags again reinforces\n    the meta-critique: each detector operates on a different signal, so no single\n    ground truth can validate all approaches. The 14.3% bimodal distribution finding\n    suggests a meaningful fraction of items have split response profiles across model\n    generations — consistent with DIF''s temporal instability finding but measuring\n    a different facet. Reviewers accept this as evidence that the contamination detection\n    space is fragmented, motivating the unified validation framework (Claim 3).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: DVD Detection Power by Contamination Type'\n    columns:\n    - Contamination type\n    - Detection rate\n    - Generation variance (mean)\n    rows:\n    - - Verbatim\n      - '0.09'\n      - '0.07'\n    - - Variant (paraphrased)\n      - '0.58'\n      - '0.41'\n    - - Clean (no contamination)\n      - '0.04'\n      - '0.34'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Distribution of generation variance scores for DVD-flagged vs unflagged\n    items, showing bimodal split in flagged items.'\n  analysis: 'DVD complements both DIF and ConStat by targeting a contamination mechanism\n    neither captures well. In the best tier, this is reported as supplementary evidence\n    for the fragmentation thesis. Like plan_003a, compressed to appendix in the 8-page\n    version. The key takeaway for reviewers is that three independent detectors with\n    Jaccard<0.20 pairwise overlap proves the field needs method-specific validation,\n    not one-size-fits-all ground truth.\n\n    '\n- name: ensemble_fusion\n  plan_id: plan_003c\n  description: 'Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble. Calibrated thresholds,\n    majority voting, weighted fusion, cross-validated weights.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    ensemble_2of3_coverage: 0.71\n    ensemble_weighted_coverage: 0.76\n    ensemble_fpr_2of3: 0.062\n    ensemble_fpr_weighted: 0.058\n    ensemble_auroc: 0.89\n    dif_solo_auroc: 0.91\n    constat_solo_auroc: 0.72\n    dvd_solo_auroc: 0.64\n    cv_weight_dif: 0.52\n    cv_weight_constat: 0.31\n    cv_weight_dvd: 0.17\n    uniform_vs_optimized_auroc_diff: 0.02\n    coverage_by_regime:\n      large_effect_dif_dominant: δ≥1.4 → DIF TPR=0.76, ensemble=0.78\n      medium_effect_constat_adds: 0.6≤δ<1.4 → DIF TPR=0.31, ensemble=0.54\n      variant_dvd_adds: variant only → DIF TPR=0.08, ensemble=0.42\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the ensemble shows that DIF alone captures\n    most of the large-effect detection power (AUROC 0.91 vs ensemble 0.89 — ensemble\n    slightly lower due to false-positive leakage from weaker signals), but the ensemble\n    provides superior coverage in the medium-effect and variant-contamination regimes\n    where DIF''s power drops below the operational threshold. The cross-validated\n    weights heavily favor DIF (0.52) reflecting its stronger signal-to-noise ratio,\n    with ConStat contributing in the SFT regime and DVD in the variant regime. The\n    key finding is that uniform-weight baseline performs within 0.02 AUROC of optimized\n    weights — the ensemble is robust to weight miscalibration. FPR<0.10 across all\n    configurations satisfies the precision constraint. However, in the best tier this\n    is NOT in the main paper — it''s appendix material. The narrative has pivoted\n    from \"DIF detects contamination\" to \"DIF characterizes benchmarks,\" making ensemble\n    detection a secondary contribution. Reviewers appreciate the thoroughness but\n    don''t require it for acceptance.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Ensemble vs Individual Detector Performance'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - AUROC\n    - Coverage@FPR<0.10\n    - Regime strength\n    rows:\n    - - DIF (MH)\n      - '0.91'\n      - '0.68'\n      - Large-effect differential\n    - - ConStat\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.44'\n      - Medium-effect, non-RL\n    - - DVD\n      - '0.64'\n      - '0.31'\n      - Variant contamination\n    - - Ensemble (2-of-3)\n      - '0.89'\n      - '0.71'\n      - Broad coverage\n    - - Ensemble (weighted)\n      - '0.89'\n      - '0.76'\n      - Broad coverage\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: ROC curves for individual detectors and ensemble, showing DIF dominating\n    at low FPR but ensemble superior at moderate FPR thresholds.'\n  analysis: 'Ensemble fusion demonstrates the complementarity thesis empirically but\n    is not load-bearing for any of the 4 claims. In the best tier, it appears in the\n    appendix as \"future direction validated in preliminary form.\" The main paper mentions\n    it in one sentence in Discussion: \"preliminary ensemble fusion (Appendix X) suggests\n    complementary coverage across detection regimes.\" This keeps the paper focused\n    on characterization rather than detection.\n\n    '\n- name: cross_benchmark_gsm8k\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: 'Replicate DIF analysis on GSM8K (METABENCH, 1,319 items × 6,702 models)\n    with GSM1K contamination gap as external validation. Three steps: temporal DIF,\n    semi-synthetic validation, and GSM1K correlation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    step1_gsm8k_temporal_dif:\n      n_items: 1319\n      n_ref: 1017\n      n_foc: 804\n      standard_c_pct: 18.9\n      external_c_pct: 47.4\n      sanity_c_pct: 0.3\n      standard_external_spearman: 0.926\n    step2_semi_synthetic:\n      standard_delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n      standard_delta_tpr_p10: 0.818\n      external_delta_tpr_p10: 0.517\n      fpr_stable: true\n    step3_gsm1k_correlation:\n      n_models: 33\n      pearson_r: -0.478\n      pearson_p: 0.005\n      pearson_bca_ci: '[-0.714, -0.155]'\n      spearman_rho: -0.403\n      spearman_p: 0.02\n      spearman_bca_ci: '[-0.687, -0.031]'\n      partial_r_controlling_accuracy: -0.385\n      partial_p: 0.03\n      partial_bca_ci: '[-0.640, -0.058]'\n      permutation_p: 0.014\n      leave_one_out_r_range: '[-0.537, -0.374]'\n      cook_d_max_model: Deepseek-math-7b-rl\n      robust: true\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the GSM8K replication serves three purposes.\n    Step 1: GSM8K''s lower C% (18.9% vs MMLU''s 31.3%) is expected — GSM8K is a single-domain\n    math benchmark with less construct heterogeneity, so fewer items exhibit differential\n    functioning. This is actually GOOD — it shows DIF rates are domain-appropriate,\n    not inflated by methodology. Step 2: ΔTPR=+0.818 (standard matching) on GSM8K\n    exceeds MMLU''s +0.674, confirming the semi-synthetic framework is portable and\n    more powerful on single-domain benchmarks where matching is cleaner. Step 3: The\n    anti-correlation r=-0.478 (p=0.005) between DIF flags and GSM1K contamination\n    gap is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence. Models with larger contamination\n    gaps (GSM8K >> GSM1K) show disproportionately LOWER performance on DIF-C items\n    — consistent with contamination producing surface gains on non-DIF items while\n    DIF items capture genuine capability. All CIs exclude zero; leave-one-out stability\n    (r range [-0.537, -0.374]) and Cook''s D analysis confirm no single influential\n    model drives the result. In the best tier, reviewers accept N=33 as suggestive\n    corroboration (not a standalone proof) because all statistical tests converge\n    and the effect direction is theoretically coherent. The wide CI upper bounds (Pearson\n    |r|=0.155, Spearman |ρ|=0.031) are honestly reported in limitations, and reviewers\n    appreciate the transparency rather than attacking it.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: GSM1K Cross-Benchmark Validation'\n    columns:\n    - Statistic\n    - Estimate\n    - p-value\n    - 95% BCa CI\n    rows:\n    - - Pearson r\n      - '-0.478'\n      - '0.005'\n      - '[-0.714, -0.155]'\n    - - Spearman ρ\n      - '-0.403'\n      - '0.020'\n      - '[-0.687, -0.031]'\n    - - Partial r (ctrl acc)\n      - '-0.385'\n      - '0.030'\n      - '[-0.640, -0.058]'\n    - - Permutation p\n      - —\n      - '0.014'\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Scatter plot of per-model DIF-C item accuracy vs GSM8K−GSM1K contamination\n    gap (N=33 models), showing negative association with regression line and 95% CI\n    band.'\n  - 'Figure: Dose-response curve for GSM8K semi-synthetic injection, showing ΔTPR\n    climbing monotonically from p=0.3 to p=1.0.'\n  analysis: 'GSM8K replication is the strongest cross-benchmark evidence in the paper.\n    In the best tier, the Step 3 correlation survives scrutiny because: (1) all four\n    statistical tests (Pearson, Spearman, partial, permutation) converge on significance,\n    (2) effect direction is theoretically predicted (DIF items resist contamination-based\n    gains), (3) leave-one-out shows robustness. The honest reporting of wide CIs and\n    N=33 limitation actually builds reviewer trust — overclaiming would trigger more\n    skepticism than underclaiming.\n\n    '\n- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: 'Pilot bifactor IRT equating on MMLU. Fit bifactor model (general +\n    57 subject-specific factors), select anchor items, fixed-parameter calibration\n    of replacements, demonstrate equated score equivalence.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    bifactor_rmsea: 0.028\n    bifactor_cfi: 0.91\n    bifactor_tli: 0.89\n    bifactor_srmr: 0.042\n    general_factor_variance_explained: 0.34\n    subject_factor_variance_explained: 0.41\n    n_anchor_items_selected: 4200\n    anchor_dif_free_pct: 100\n    equated_rho_original_vs_replaced: 0.964\n    equated_tau_original_vs_replaced: 0.951\n    calibration_convergence_rate: 0.92\n    mean_equating_error_theta: 0.08\n    max_equating_error_theta: 0.31\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the bifactor model achieves acceptable (not\n    excellent) fit — RMSEA=0.028 is good, but CFI=0.91 and TLI=0.89 are at the conventional\n    threshold, reflecting MMLU''s inherent 57-domain multidimensionality that no simple\n    factor structure fully captures. The general factor explains only 34% of variance,\n    with subject-specific factors at 41% — consistent with MMLU being more of a test\n    battery than a unidimensional measure. This is itself an important finding: it\n    empirically quantifies why unidimensional IRT is misspecified for MMLU (explaining\n    the |Lz|>2 misfit observed in 98% of models). The equated rankings achieve ρ=0.964\n    (above the ≥0.95 target), demonstrating that psychometric equating is feasible\n    even with imperfect model fit. In the best tier, this pilot is reported in 2-3\n    sentences in Discussion as \"bifactor equating is feasible; full development is\n    future work\" rather than as a standalone claim. The marginal fit metrics are honestly\n    reported and positioned as motivation for multidimensional IRT extensions.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Bifactor IRT Fit Indices'\n    columns:\n    - Index\n    - Value\n    - Threshold\n    - Verdict\n    rows:\n    - - RMSEA\n      - '0.028'\n      - <0.05\n      - Good\n    - - CFI\n      - '0.91'\n      - '>0.95'\n      - Marginal\n    - - TLI\n      - '0.89'\n      - '>0.95'\n      - Marginal\n    - - SRMR\n      - '0.042'\n      - <0.08\n      - Good\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Scatter plot of equated θ (bifactor-anchored) vs original θ (raw total\n    score) for 5,219 models, showing tight correlation with ρ=0.964.'\n  analysis: 'Bifactor results serve as proof-of-concept for the equating pipeline,\n    not as a polished contribution. In the best tier, the marginal CFI/TLI values\n    are a feature, not a bug — they empirically demonstrate that MMLU requires multidimensional\n    treatment, supporting the limitations section''s call for future MIRT work. The\n    successful equating despite imperfect fit shows the approach is promising enough\n    to warrant dedicated follow-up.\n\n    '\n- name: item_generation_calibration\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: 'Generate replacement items for DIF-flagged MMLU questions using LLM\n    generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation, validate\n    difficulty correlation.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_generated: 500\n    n_dif_flagged_targeted: 200\n    generation_quality_human_rating: 3.8\n    difficulty_corr_prompt_based_rho: 0.32\n    difficulty_corr_student_sim_rho: 0.71\n    difficulty_corr_empirical_rho: 0.68\n    construct_alignment_pct: 0.84\n    cognitive_level_match_pct: 0.78\n    holdout_panel_rho: 0.65\n    generation_reject_rate: 0.22\n    avg_generation_attempts: 1.8\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, LLM-generated replacement items achieve reasonable\n    but imperfect quality. The key finding is the gap between prompt-based difficulty\n    targeting (ρ=0.32, consistent with literature''s 0.10-0.45) and student simulation\n    calibration (ρ=0.71) — simulation doubles the difficulty prediction accuracy,\n    validating the calibration approach. The empirical correlation (ρ=0.68) slightly\n    lags simulation prediction, reflecting real-world noise that simulation doesn''t\n    fully capture. Construct alignment (84%) and cognitive level matching (78%) are\n    adequate for a pilot but below production quality, honestly reported. In the best\n    tier, this appears as 1-2 sentences in Future Work: \"preliminary item generation\n    with student simulation calibration achieves ρ=0.71 difficulty correlation, suggesting\n    automated benchmark maintenance is feasible.\" Reviewers don''t evaluate this as\n    a standalone contribution — it''s a forward-looking demonstration.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Item Difficulty Prediction Accuracy'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - ρ with empirical difficulty\n    - 95% CI\n    rows:\n    - - Prompt-based targeting\n      - '0.32'\n      - '[0.19, 0.44]'\n    - - Student simulation\n      - '0.71'\n      - '[0.64, 0.77]'\n    - - Empirical (holdout panel)\n      - '0.68'\n      - '[0.60, 0.75]'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Calibration plot of simulated vs empirical item difficulty for 200 generated\n    replacement items, with ρ=0.71 and 45-degree reference line.'\n  analysis: 'Item generation is explicitly positioned as future work in the best tier.\n    The ρ=0.71 result is promising enough to motivate follow-up but not strong enough\n    to claim as a contribution. In the EMNLP submission, this is at most an appendix\n    entry demonstrating the practical viability of the DIF-informed benchmark maintenance\n    pipeline.\n\n    '\n- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: 'Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al., 2006) over\n    4 successive benchmark administrations. Show power accumulation for medium effects.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    admin_1_power_d10: 0.38\n    admin_2_power_d10: 0.54\n    admin_3_power_d10: 0.69\n    admin_4_power_d10: 0.78\n    admin_1_power_d14: 0.68\n    admin_2_power_d14: 0.82\n    admin_3_power_d14: 0.91\n    admin_4_power_d14: 0.95\n    cumulative_fpr_admin4: 0.048\n    bayes_factor_threshold: 3.0\n    prior_update_mechanism: posterior from admin t → prior for admin t+1\n    simulation_n_items: 1000\n    simulation_n_models_per_admin: 200\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the cumulative Bayesian approach shows clean\n    power accumulation: from 0.38 (single administration, δ=1.0) to 0.69 after 3 administrations,\n    crossing the 0.70 power threshold. At δ=1.4 (the operational threshold identified\n    in power simulations), power reaches 0.91 by administration 3 and 0.95 by administration\n    4. The FPR remains controlled at 0.048 (below 0.05) even after 4 accumulation\n    rounds, confirming the Bayesian framework properly regularizes false positives.\n    This is a simulation-only result — no real multi-wave longitudinal data exists\n    for LLM benchmarks. In the best tier, this is purely appendix material: \"Simulation\n    shows cumulative Bayesian DIF achieves 0.69 power at δ=1.0 after 3 administrations,\n    suggesting longitudinal benchmark monitoring is feasible.\" Reviewers see it as\n    nice-to-have future work, not a current contribution.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Cumulative Power by Administration Wave'\n    columns:\n    - Administration\n    - Power (δ=1.0)\n    - Power (δ=1.4)\n    - FPR\n    rows:\n    - - '1'\n      - '0.38'\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.042'\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.54'\n      - '0.82'\n      - '0.044'\n    - - '3'\n      - '0.69'\n      - '0.91'\n      - '0.046'\n    - - '4'\n      - '0.78'\n      - '0.95'\n      - '0.048'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Power curves across 4 administrations for δ∈{0.6, 0.8, 1.0, 1.2, 1.4,\n    1.6}, showing monotonic accumulation with diminishing returns after administration\n    3.'\n  analysis: 'Cumulative Bayesian DIF is the most forward-looking experiment in the\n    plan. In the best tier, it provides the theoretical foundation for the Conclusion''s\n    recommendation that \"benchmark maintainers should run DIF audits periodically\"\n    — by showing that cumulative monitoring outperforms repeated one-shot testing.\n    Entirely simulation-based and relegated to appendix, but it gives the paper a\n    constructive vision beyond diagnosis.\n\n    '\n- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: 'Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement.\n    Simulate 5-50% replacement rates, measure ranking stability, reliability, coverage.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    replacement_5pct_rho: 0.999\n    replacement_10pct_rho: 0.998\n    replacement_20pct_rho: 0.994\n    replacement_50pct_rho: 0.976\n    reliability_shr_original: 0.993\n    reliability_shr_5pct: 0.992\n    reliability_shr_20pct: 0.987\n    reliability_shr_50pct: 0.969\n    construct_coverage_original: 1.0\n    construct_coverage_20pct: 0.96\n    construct_coverage_50pct: 0.88\n    theoretical_bound_rho_lower: ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (r = replacement fraction)\n    critical_replacement_threshold: 0.35\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, graceful degradation shows that MMLU''s massive\n    redundancy (already revealed by plan_017''s ρ≈0.997 after 31% DIF removal) translates\n    into a wide safe zone for item replacement. Up to 20% replacement, ranking stability\n    stays above 0.99 and reliability above 0.98. The critical threshold at 35% marks\n    where construct coverage drops below 0.95 — interestingly close to the 31% DIF\n    rate, suggesting that replacing all DIF items simultaneously would be at the edge\n    of the safe zone. This is a useful calibration result: benchmark maintainers can\n    replace DIF items in waves (10-15% per refresh) without compromising measurement\n    properties. The theoretical bound ρ ≥ 1 - 0.12·r² (where r = replacement fraction)\n    provides a practical planning tool. In the best tier, this appears as 2-3 sentences\n    in Discussion supporting the recommendation that DIF-informed benchmark refresh\n    is operationally safe.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Validity Degradation Under Progressive Replacement'\n    columns:\n    - Replacement %\n    - ρ (ranking)\n    - SHR (reliability)\n    - Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - 0% (baseline)\n      - '1.000'\n      - '0.993'\n      - '1.00'\n    - - 5%\n      - '0.999'\n      - '0.992'\n      - '0.99'\n    - - 10%\n      - '0.998'\n      - '0.990'\n      - '0.98'\n    - - 20%\n      - '0.994'\n      - '0.987'\n      - '0.96'\n    - - 31% (all DIF)\n      - '0.989'\n      - '0.982'\n      - '0.93'\n    - - 50%\n      - '0.976'\n      - '0.969'\n      - '0.88'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Three-panel degradation curves (ρ, SHR, coverage) as function of replacement\n    fraction, with vertical line at 31% (DIF rate) and shaded safe zone.'\n  analysis: 'Graceful degradation complements plan_017''s ranking stability finding\n    by extending it to the replacement scenario. In the best tier, this empirically\n    grounds the practical recommendation in the Conclusion. The 35% critical threshold\n    being near the 31% DIF rate creates a natural narrative: \"the current level of\n    instability is at the edge of MMLU''s redundancy buffer — one more model generation\n    may exceed it.\" This urgency framing, backed by quantitative bounds, strengthens\n    the paper''s call to action without being alarmist.\n\n    '\n- name: item_focused_trees\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: 'Implement Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger, 2016) as alternative\n    to categorical cohort DIF. Model release date as continuous covariate, compare\n    with MH-DIF.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n    ift_significant_temporal_pct: 34.7\n    ift_vs_mh_c_jaccard: 0.72\n    ift_mh_concordance_rate: 0.81\n    ift_additional_detections: 423\n    ift_missed_by_mh: 0\n    ift_optimal_split_date: '2023-09-15'\n    ift_nonlinear_items_pct: 8.3\n    ift_computation_time_hours: 2.4\n    ift_vs_mh_rank_corr_delta_mh: 0.88\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, Item-Focused Trees confirm the MH-DIF findings\n    while adding nuance. The 34.7% IFT-significant rate slightly exceeds MH''s 31.3%\n    because IFT captures gradual temporal trends that MH''s binary split misses, plus\n    8.3% of items show nonlinear temporal patterns (e.g., stability→instability→restabilization).\n    The high concordance (Jaccard=0.72, concordance=0.81) validates MH''s simpler\n    approach for the primary analysis, while IFT''s additional detections (423 items)\n    represent borderline cases near MH''s threshold. The optimal split date (2023-09-15)\n    aligns with the ChatGPT-era capability jump, providing temporal specificity that\n    MH''s cohort-level analysis cannot. Zero missed detections (IFT ⊃ MH-C) confirms\n    MH is conservative, supporting the \"31% is a lower bound\" framing. In the best\n    tier, IFT appears as a robustness check in appendix: \"continuous-covariate IFT\n    analysis confirms the temporal DIF pattern (concordance=0.81) and identifies the\n    optimal split near 2023-09, consistent with known capability transitions.\"\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: IFT vs MH-DIF Concordance'\n    columns:\n    - Category\n    - MH-C items\n    - IFT-significant items\n    - Overlap\n    rows:\n    - - Both flagged\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 3495\n    - - MH-C only\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 0\n    - - IFT only\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 423\n    - - Neither\n      - —\n      - —\n      - 8590\n    - - Concordance\n      - —\n      - —\n      - '0.81'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Item-level temporal DIF trajectory for 3 exemplar items, showing IFT''s\n    continuous covariate function vs MH''s binary flag, with release date on x-axis.'\n  analysis: 'IFT provides methodological depth and confirms the MH analysis is appropriate.\n    In the best tier, the zero-miss result (MH-C ⊂ IFT) is particularly valuable —\n    it means MH''s simpler approach doesn''t miss important signals, just captures\n    fewer borderline cases. This defuses any reviewer concern about arbitrary cohort\n    splitting. Relegated to appendix as robustness check.\n\n    '\n- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: 'Run DIF within architecture-homogeneous subpopulations. Subset to\n    decoder-only models, compare IRT fit and DIF detection vs full population.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_decoder_only: 5226\n    n_non_decoder: 1\n    decoder_only_pct: 99.98\n    c_pct_decoder_only: 31.3\n    c_pct_full: 31.3\n    jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full: 1.0\n    delta_mh_rank_corr: 0.9999\n    irt_rmsea_full: 0.089\n    irt_rmsea_decoder_only: 0.088\n    irt_lz_pct_above_2_full: 98.1\n    irt_lz_pct_above_2_decoder_only: 98.0\n    removed_model: RWKV-based (1 model)\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the architecture stratification result is maximally\n    clean: removing the single non-decoder-only model (RWKV) changes absolutely nothing\n    (Jaccard=1.0, identical C%). This is because METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only\n    — architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue by construction. The IRT fit metrics\n    barely change because the |Lz|>2 misfit reflects MMLU''s 57-domain multidimensionality,\n    not architecture mixing. This result preempts the \"spurious DIF from architecture\n    heterogeneity\" concern in a single sentence: \"METABENCH MMLU contains 5,226/5,227\n    decoder-only models; excluding the 1 non-decoder model yields identical DIF flags\n    (Jaccard=1.0).\" In the best tier, reviewers see this preemptive control as a sign\n    of thoroughness. The persistent |Lz|>2 is discussed in limitations as inherent\n    to applying 1D IRT to a multidimensional test battery — not a methodological failure\n    but a known constraint motivating future MIRT work.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Architecture Stratification Impact'\n    columns:\n    - Population\n    - N\n    - C%\n    - Jaccard vs full\n    - RMSEA\n    rows:\n    - - Full (all architectures)\n      - 5227\n      - '31.3'\n      - —\n      - '0.089'\n    - - Decoder-only\n      - 5226\n      - '31.3'\n      - '1.0'\n      - '0.088'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: ΔMH scatter plot (full vs decoder-only) for 12,508 items — perfect correlation\n    with all points on the identity line.'\n  analysis: 'Architecture stratification is the simplest experiment in the plan but\n    one of the most valuable rhetorically. In the best tier, the Jaccard=1.0 result\n    is a one-line knockout of a potential reviewer concern. It also reveals an important\n    meta-insight: the METABENCH MMLU ecosystem is architecturally homogeneous, so\n    any observed DIF must reflect temporal/training differences, not architectural\n    ones. This eliminates an entire class of confounds.\n\n    '\n- name: semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: 'Inject known contamination into real METABENCH response matrix. Controlled\n    ground truth for DIF detection power. Vary injection prevalence, exploitation\n    rate, focal group size.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    temporal_cohort_results:\n      baseline_fpr: 0.314\n      tpr_p00: 0.326\n      tpr_p03: 0.548\n      tpr_p05: 0.834\n      tpr_p07: 0.99\n      tpr_p10: 1.0\n      delta_tpr_p05: 0.508\n      delta_tpr_p10: 0.674\n      fpr_inflation_at_p10: 0.302\n    ability_cohort_results:\n      baseline_fpr: 0.493\n      tpr_p00: 0.498\n      tpr_p10: 0.8\n      delta_tpr_p10: 0.302\n    external_matching_results:\n      fpr: 0.314\n      tpr_p05: 0.834\n      tpr_p10: 1.0\n      fpr_stable_across_injection: true\n    cross_subject_matching_results:\n      delta_fpr_all_injection_levels: 0.0\n      tpr_improvement_over_within: 4/5 subjects improved\n    dose_response_clean: true\n    exploitation_rate_sensitivity:\n      p04_tpr: 0.62\n      p06_tpr: 0.91\n      p08_tpr: 0.98\n  result_rationale: 'In the best tier, the semi-synthetic validation provides the\n    controlled ground truth that proxy labels cannot. The ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5 demonstrates\n    substantial detection power: injecting 50% exploitation rate into 200 items produces\n    a +0.508 increase in true positive rate above the temporal baseline. The dose-response\n    curve is clean and monotonic — from p=0.3 (ΔTPR≈0.22) through p=0.5 (+0.508) to\n    p=1.0 (+0.674) — confirming that DIF detection power scales predictably with contamination\n    intensity. The cross-subject matching innovation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all injection\n    levels) is the methodological contribution: it eliminates the FPR inflation that\n    plagues within-subject matching (which inflates FPR by +0.186 because the matching\n    variable itself becomes contaminated). This finding has practical value — any\n    future DIF application to multi-domain benchmarks should use cross-subject matching.\n    The ability cohort shows weaker ΔTPR (+0.302) because the baseline FPR is already\n    high (0.493), leaving less room for improvement. In the best tier, the bug fix\n    (inf-delta classified as extreme DIF not class A) is transparently reported as\n    a methodological note, increasing reviewer trust. The matching variable contamination\n    finding is recognized as a subtle and important methodological insight.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table: Semi-Synthetic Validation — Dose-Response'\n    columns:\n    - Exploitation rate (p)\n    - TPR (temporal)\n    - ΔTPR\n    - FPR (external match)\n    rows:\n    - - 0.0 (baseline)\n      - '0.326'\n      - —\n      - '0.314'\n    - - '0.3'\n      - '0.548'\n      - '+0.222'\n      - '0.314'\n    - - '0.5'\n      - '0.834'\n      - '+0.508'\n      - '0.314'\n    - - '0.7'\n      - '0.990'\n      - '+0.664'\n      - '0.314'\n    - - '1.0'\n      - '1.000'\n      - '+0.674'\n      - '0.314'\n  - name: 'Table: Matching Strategy Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Matching strategy\n    - ΔFPR (p=1.0)\n    - ΔTPR (p=0.5)\n    - Verdict\n    rows:\n    - - Standard (within-subject)\n      - '+0.302'\n      - '+0.508'\n      - FPR inflated\n    - - External (cross-subject)\n      - '0.000'\n      - '+0.508'\n      - FPR controlled ✓\n    - - Cross-benchmark (GSM8K)\n      - '0.000'\n      - '+0.818'\n      - FPR controlled ✓\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Dose-response curves for temporal and ability cohorts, showing ΔTPR as\n    function of exploitation rate p, with shaded 95% bootstrap CIs.'\n  - 'Figure: FPR comparison across matching strategies at increasing injection levels,\n    showing external matching stable at 0.314 while standard matching inflates to\n    0.616.'\n  analysis: 'The semi-synthetic framework is the paper''s methodological contribution\n    to the field — Claim 3 rests entirely on this experiment. In the best tier, the\n    ΔTPR metric is recognized as a principled way to evaluate any item-level contamination\n    detector under controlled conditions, and the cross-subject matching insight is\n    appreciated as a practical recommendation with general applicability. The dose-response\n    linearity gives practitioners intuitive guidance: \"at 50% exploitation, DIF detects\n    with ΔTPR≈0.5.\" The FPR=0.314 baseline (reflecting the 31.3% temporal DIF rate)\n    is not a false positive problem — it''s the true temporal instability rate, which\n    the injected contamination adds to. Reviewers in the best tier understand this\n    distinction.'\n","numLines":1052,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1052}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014k8KC94UWXy3mevuRnqDjt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've read the full PDF (9 pages) and the review_context.yaml. Now I need to execute the full dream-review process:\n\n1. Dispatch 2 reviewers in parallel (R1: methods+experiments, R2: novelty+related work)\n2. Run the full roundtable (Step 1→4)\n3. Submit the review report\n\nLet me first load the submit tool, then dispatch both reviewers in parallel.\n\nLet me think about what each reviewer should focus on:\n\n**R1 (Methods + Experiments):** \n\n\nEvaluating the soundness of applying MH-DIF methodology to LLM benchmarks, checking the validity of how they defined cohorts temporally and by ability, assessing their semi-synthetic validation framework with dose-response and matching strategies, verifying cross-benchmark validation across GSM8K and GSM1K, examining DIF as a diagnostic tool, ensuring statistical rigor with multiple testing corrections and bootstrap confidence intervals, and comparing the appendix experiments against their original experiment plan.\n\n**R2 (Novelty + Related Work):**\nAssessing how novel it is to apply DIF to LLM benchmarks, checking whether they adequately covered related work, and evaluating the strength of their meta-critique.\n\nNow I need to consider how this work positions itself against contamination detection literature, whether the conceptual contribution that instability doesn't necessarily mean invalidity holds up, and checking the writing quality, clarity, and EMNLP format compliance before sending this to reviewers.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39056,"cache_read_input_tokens":24130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39056},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":n