{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_012J8qs85SvBnfGWR4zV2UTh","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section*{Limitations}\n2\t\n3\t\\paragraph{Methodological scope of MH-DIF.}\n4\tThe Mantel-Haenszel procedure detects only \\emph{uniform} DIF, where the odds ratio between cohorts is constant across ability strata. Logistic regression DIF analysis reveals an additional 24.8\\% of items exhibiting non-uniform DIF ($|\\beta_{\\mathrm{interaction}}| \\geq 0.5$), making the 31\\% ETS~C estimate conservative. Future work should apply non-uniform DIF methods (e.g., logistic regression or IRT-based approaches) as the primary analysis to capture the full spectrum of item instability.\n5\t\n6\t\\paragraph{Ability confounding.}\n7\tAlthough the temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (Cohen's $d = -0.17$, overlap $= 76.4\\%$) and nearest-neighbor matching confirms that at most 2.4 percentage points of C\\% are attributable to ability mismatch, within-family comparisons with large ability gaps (e.g., Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3, $d = -0.99$) produce substantially inflated C\\% (61.3\\% unmatched vs.\\ 28.3\\% matched). Any application of MH-DIF to cohorts with larger ability differences must incorporate ability matching or stratification beyond the standard 5-strata procedure.\n8\t\n9\t\\paragraph{Sample size requirements.}\n10\tOur power analysis indicates reliable ETS~C detection requires $N \\geq 80$ per cohort. The temporal cohorts (1,080 and 807 models) far exceed this threshold, but the GSM1K correlation analysis ($N = 33$) has limited statistical power (95\\% CI $= [-0.71, -0.16]$). The negative correlation between contamination gap and DIF ranking advantage ($r = -0.478$) is exploratory and warrants replication with larger model samples.\n11\t\n12\t\\paragraph{Contamination vs.\\ capability change.}\n13\tDIF analysis detects \\emph{that} items function differently across cohorts but cannot determine \\emph{why}. The differential signal may reflect contamination, capability change, training methodology shifts, or interactions among these factors. We present evidence that DIF captures something distinct from memorization (negative GSM1K correlation, semi-synthetic dose-response), but a definitive causal decomposition remains out of reach without access to model training data.\n14\t\n15\t\\paragraph{Benchmark scope.}\n16\tWe analyze MMLU (12,508 items) and GSM8K (1,319 items), both multiple-choice or short-answer benchmarks scored as binary correct/incorrect. The generalizability of our findings to open-ended generation benchmarks (e.g., AlpacaEval, MT-Bench), coding benchmarks (e.g., HumanEval), or benchmarks with non-binary scoring remains untested. The binary response format is a prerequisite for MH-DIF; extending DIF analysis to continuous or partial-credit scoring would require ordinal or polytomous DIF methods.\n17\t\n18\t\\paragraph{Temporal cohort definition.}\n19\tWe define cohorts by calendar year of model release (2023 vs.\\ 2024), a coarse grouping that aggregates models with diverse training pipelines, data mixtures, and post-training procedures. Finer-grained cohort definitions (e.g., by training data cutoff, model family, or alignment method) might reveal more targeted patterns of item instability but would reduce per-cohort sample sizes and statistical power.\n20\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_016F2DjFz7CR4NBnYYyK5kUd","session_id":"0387b3d5-0b40-4998-a89e-ed7775c5671d","uuid":"48043789-79ef-4c33-949e-a72eb8210474","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:33:56.901Z"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_014k8KC94UWXy3mevuRnqDjt","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013wHGCkM5iwQVzh5xDirnTk","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) for an EMNLP 2026 submission. Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**. \n\n## Venue calibration\nEMNLP 2026 via ARR. Overall Assessment scale: 5=Award (top 2.5%), 4=Conference accept, 3=Findings, 2=Resubmit, 1=Do not resubmit. Accept rate ~21%. Dimensions: Soundness (1-5), Excitement (1-5), Reproducibility (1-5). Auto-reject: anonymity violation, over page limit, missing Limitations, hallucinated citations.\n\n## Paper summary\nTitle: \"The Missing Diagnostic Layer: A Large-Scale Psychometric Audit of LLM Benchmark Temporal Stability\"\n\nThe paper applies Mantel–Haenszel DIF analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) to assess temporal stability of MMLU. \n\n**4 claims:**\n1. 31.3% of MMLU items show large DIF (ETS C) between 2023 and 2024-H1 cohorts. Robust to thresholds, multiple testing, ability confounding, architecture.\n2. Web-overlap ground truth (Li et al.) cannot capture differential signals — enrichment≈1.0 is expected, not failure. Meta-critique of validation paradigm.\n3. Semi-synthetic validation framework: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000), portable to GSM8K (ΔTPR=+0.818).\n4. DIF as diagnostic lens: items carry cross-cohort signal yet are least disruptive non-random removal (ρ≈0.997). GSM1K correlation r=−0.478 (p=0.005).\n\n**Main paper (8 pages):** Sections 1-7 (Intro, Related Work, Method, Experiments, Discussion, Limitations, Conclusion) + Tables 1-6 + References on page 8.\n**Appendices A-I (pages 6-8 area):** RL robustness, ConStat eval, DVD eval, Ensemble fusion, Bifactor IRT, Item generation, Cumulative Bayesian DIF, Graceful degradation, Item-Focused Trees.\n\n## Related Work section content (page 2):\n- **Contamination detection**: Min-K% [Shi 2024], n-gram overlap [Oren 2024], ConStat [2024], DVD [2024]. Per-model detectors validated against web-overlap/n-gram ground truth. Meta-critique applies.\n- **Benchmark dynamics**: Growing Pains [2024] identifies anchor items. LiveBench [2024] and dynamic benchmarks sidestep via refresh. Paper provides the diagnostic layer for knowing *which* items to refresh.\n- **DIF in NLP**: Rupp & Zumbo [2006], Zumbo [2007] — fairness auditing, bias detection. Not applied to temporal stability of LLM benchmarks.\n- **IRT for LLMs**: Polo et al. [2024], Raju et al. [2024] — ability estimation focus, not measurement invariance.\n\n## References (page 8-9):\nHendrycks et al. 2021, Holland & Thayer 1988, METABENCH Team 2024, Li et al. 2024, Shi et al. 2024, Oren et al. 2024, ConStat Authors 2024, DVD Authors 2024, Growing Pains Authors 2024, LiveBench Authors 2024, Rupp & Zumbo 2006, Zumbo 2007, Polo et al. 2024, Raju et al. 2024, Sinharay et al. 2006, Tutz & Berger 2016.\n\nTotal: 16 references.\n\n## Key paper claims on novelty:\n- \"First large-scale psychometric audit of LLM benchmark temporal stability\"\n- \"First to treat model cohorts as 'examinee groups' in the DIF framework\"\n- Meta-critique: web-overlap ground truth is fundamentally mismatched for item-level differential detectors\n- Cross-subject matching as methodological contribution\n- \"Instability ≠ invalidity\" framing as conceptual contribution\n\n## Your task\n\nProvide a detailed R2 review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment**: How novel is applying DIF to LLM benchmarks? Is the conceptual contribution (\"instability ≠ invalidity\", \"missing diagnostic layer\") genuine or incremental? Is the meta-critique of web-overlap ground truth insightful?\n2. **Related work completeness**: Are there missing references? Key papers in contamination detection, benchmark evaluation, psychometrics/IRT for LLMs, or DIF methodology that should be cited?\n3. **Positioning**: Is the paper well-positioned relative to existing work? Does it clearly differentiate from Growing Pains, LiveBench, and other benchmark dynamics papers? Is the relationship to ConStat/DVD/Min-K% clearly established?\n4. **Claim novelty vs. execution**: Does the novelty come from the idea (applying DIF) or the execution (scale, controls, meta-critique)? Is there prior work that diminishes the novelty?\n5. **Writing quality**: Is the paper well-written? Is the 8-page compression successful? Are the claims clearly stated and supported?\n6. **Presentation**: Are figures/tables effective? Is the paper organized well? Is the appendix well-structured?\n7. **EMNLP fit**: Is this appropriate for EMNLP? Does it fit the NLP evaluation / resources track?\n8. **Anonymity check**: Any deanonymization concerns?\n9. **Citation quality**: Any suspicious references (placeholder names like \"ConStat Authors\", \"DVD Authors\", \"Growing Pains Authors\", \"LiveBench Authors\")? Do all citations appear real?\n\n## Output format\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentences]\n\n### Strengths\nS1. ...\nS2. ...\n...\n\n### Weaknesses\nW1. ...\nW2. ...\n...\n\n### Questions for Authors\nQ1. ...\nQ2. ...\n...\n\n### Missing References\n- ...\n\n### Minor Issues\n- ...\n\n### Preliminary Scores\n- Soundness: X/5\n- Excitement: X/5\n- Reproducibility: X/5\n- Overall: X.X/5\n- Confidence: X/5\n```\n\nBe rigorous. Calibrate to EMNLP acceptance rate (~21%). A score of 4.0 means you believe this should be accepted at the main conference.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":39056,"cache_read_input_tokens":24130,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":39056},"output_tokens":22,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0387b3d5-0b40-4998-a89e-ed7775c5671d","uuid":"715f298e-43bd-468d-a371-1776dc60d586"}
